window.CET4_BANKS.push({"id": "2025-12-2", "source": "2025年12月真题·第2套", "writing": {"prompt": "Directions: Suppose it is proposed that all university students should be required to learn academic writing. You are now to write a response by stating what you think of the proposal. You will have 30 minutes to write the essay. You should write at least 120 words but no more than 180 words.", "sample": "It has recently been proposed that all university students should be required to learn academic writing. In my\nview, this proposal deserves our full support, for such a skill is far more than an optional refinement.\nFor one thing, academic writing trains students to organize ideas logically and cite sources honestly. When I\nwrote my first term paper, I struggled to structure arguments and nearly copied online material by mistake; a\nwriting course would have taught me both clear reasoning and academic integrity. For another, this ability pays\noff well beyond campus. Whether a graduate applies for a research position or drafts a report at work, the\ncapacity to express thoughts precisely on paper often determines success.\nAdmittedly, mastering academic writing takes time and effort. Nevertheless, the long-term rewards clearly\noutweigh the cost. Therefore, I strongly believe universities should make this course compulsory, ideally\nstarting from the first year so that students benefit throughout their studies."}, "translation": {"prompt": "近年来，\"城市漫步(city walk)\"在中国的年轻人中悄然兴起，已成为一种旅游新潮流。与传统旅游不同，城市漫步不追求在短时间内游览尽可能多的景点。而是随意行走或按照主题路线漫步在城市的大街小巷。漫步者品尝地道小吃，欣赏特色建筑，了解当地生活方式。他们还用相机或手机记录城市的风景和人物。这种旅游方式能让城市漫步者更深入了解城市的历史与文化。", "sample": "In recent years, city walk has quietly emerged among young people in China and has become a new travel trend. Unlike traditional travel, city walk does not seek to visit as many attractions as possible in a short time. Instead, city walkers wander randomly or stroll along themed routes through the streets and alleys of the city. They taste authentic snacks, admire distinctive buildings, and learn about local ways of life. They also record the city's scenery and people with cameras or mobile phones. This travel style enables city walkers to gain a deeper understanding of the city's history and culture."}, "listeningUrl": "https://english-exam.lazynote.cn/cet4/paper/2025-12-2/?f=w", "clozePassage": "Some music inspires you to move your feet, some inspires you to get out there and change the world. In any case, it's  __26__  to say that music moves people in special ways.\nIf you're especially into a piece of music, your brain does something called Autonomous Sensory Meridian Response (ASMR), which  __27__  to you like a sting in your brain.\nIt turns out that ASMR is pretty special. According to a  __28__  published study in The Journal of Prevention of Alzheimer's Disease, the part of your brain responsible for ASMR doesn't get lost to Alzheimer's. Alzheimer's  __29__  to put people into layers of confusion, and the study confirms that music can sometimes  __30__  lift people out of the Alzheimer's haze and bring them back to (at least a likeness of) normality … if only for a short while. ASMR is powerful stuff!\nThis phenomenon has been  __31__  several times but rarely studied properly. One of the most famous examples of this is the story of 92-year-old Henry Dryer, who comes out of dementia(痴呆) while listening to songs from his youth.\nJeff Anderson, associate professor in Radiology at the University of Utah and  __32__  author on the study, says \"In our society, the diagnoses of dementia are  __33__  resources to the maximum. No one says playing music will be a cure for Alzheimer's disease, but it might make the symptoms more  __34__  , decrease the cost of care and improve a patient's  __35__  of life.\"", "matchIntro": "Can playing video games help you get a better job?", "matchParagraphs": [{"label": "A", "text": "The gaming industry is larger than films and music combined, yet few of us are likely to list our achievements in playing computer games as work experience in our résumé. But why not? Businesses are waking up to the skills gamers can bring to the workplace."}, {"label": "B", "text": "One start-up is convinced that the skills learned by playing games — hard-won through years of training and battle — can be applied to real-life work situations. And Game Academy reckons its belief is backed up by hard data. Do you enjoy unusual puzzle games like Portal, or tower defence games like Defense Grid? The team has found that IT workers play those more than average. But if you prefer Civilization, Total War, or X-Com, where strategy and resource management are key, then you might have more in common with managers."}, {"label": "C", "text": "Game Academy's idea is simple: analyse gamers' habits from their online gaming profile, and offer courses in valuable skills that reflect their aptitudes — skills they can practise and refine in-game. And there is already a growing acceptance that gaming skills are transferable."}, {"label": "D", "text": "Even the military is hiring gamers. \"The ability to absorb information, react swiftly and coordinate actions whilst remaining calm under pressure are often attributes of people that are good at gaming,\" according to a Royal Air Force spokesperson. Those skills are part of what the Royal Air Force is looking for \"in a variety of roles\". \"Skills acquired through gaming can be very relevant to certain areas.\""}, {"label": "E", "text": "\"There are plenty of soft skills that gamers can utilise in a professional setting, such as teamwork, problem solving and strategic planning,\" says Ryan Gardner, a regional director with Hays recruitment. But does that really mean your top 100 ranking in Overwatch should go down in your résumé? \"It's about how you either make it relevant to the job you are applying for, or how it makes you more interesting as a potential employee,\" Mr Gardner says."}, {"label": "F", "text": "Two years ago, a Glasgow University study made headlines for suggesting gaming could make students more successful. But \"the research hasn't really changed minds, at least not yet,\" says one of the authors of the study, Dr Matthew Barr. He's now writing a book on the topic. \"For now, I think the negative reputation around games means that telling someone you're a determined gamer is more likely to harm your chances of getting a job. But if someone can explain how they've led a team in an online game, for example, they may be able to persuade an employer that this is useful,\" says Dr Barr."}, {"label": "G", "text": "Playing games casually is unlikely to help your career prospects. But part of Game Academy's suggestion is to transform casual gamers into \"conscious\" ones, applying critical thinking to developing their skills. \"We see gameplay as a resource of talent,\" says co-founder David Barrie. \"Why can't we put in our résumé our gaming victories and achievements?\" he asks. \"If they say they want leadership — why can't I point to my years of leading World of Warcraft raids?\""}, {"label": "H", "text": "There are already plenty of gamers out there who know that their hobby has made them who they are. Earlier this year, Matthew Ricci told gaming site Kotaku: \"If you're playing EVE Online you basically already have an MBA.\" Often made fun of in gaming circles as a \"spreadsheet simulator(电子表格模拟)\", the economy of the fictional EVE universe is driven by real market principles. If you want to build a new spaceship, the raw material has to be mined by another player. Manufacturing costs come into effect, and commodities fluctuate in price based on demand and the distance to haul the commodity."}, {"label": "I", "text": "Mr Ricci, who had always dreamed of being the boss of his own company, ran an in-game corporation comprising hundreds of players. Eventually, he realised he could transfer his skills to real-life business — instead of doing it for free. He restructured Zentech, once a taxation vehicle for his father's business, and it is now in its fourth year helping international brands enter the Canadian market. EVE teaches skills like creativity, leadership, organisation, and conflict resolution, he maintains. He credits his success to his family, his strong desire to run his own business — and \"a damn good company in Iceland that made a damn good game\"."}, {"label": "J", "text": "Digital entrepreneur and business consultant Mia Bennett says: \"In more traditional settings, gaming is still imagined to be the pursuit of teenage boys — a waste of time.\" But there are some links to skills like \"decision-making, the ability to anticipate and future planning,\" she says. \"It also helps with meta-skills — learning how to learn, experimentation, and creative thinking.\""}, {"label": "K", "text": "Twelve years into his career, Mark Long, a radiotherapy physicist with the NHS in Surrey, doesn't get as much gaming time as he used to. \"I would love to say that my gaming skills allow me to destroy cancer using linear accelerators like photon(光子) blasters, but that's not quite the reality.\" Instead, he credits old-school games like Palace of Magic with exposing him to computers. Every new gaming upgrade improved his knowledge of how they worked. Gaming also encouraged a competitive spirit — and that, he believes, translates. \"When creating treatment plans, the aim is to optimise the radiation dose to the tumour and restrict it as much as possible to healthy surrounding tissues and organs,\" he says. It's about \"repeating the process, but each time doing something slightly different to improve the result\". Not unlike achieving a high score or a perfect run. \"Most videogames are essentially puzzles to solve,\" he says. \"And problem-solving is a big part of my job.\""}, {"label": "L", "text": "\"Over at Game Academy, Mr Barrie is aware they still have a mountain to climb. The employer community will need the science,\" he says, but confesses he only became a true believer when he started asking players about their biggest in-game achievements. \"The scale and scope of their answers were amazing,\" he says. \"But ask them the same about their work, and they don't know.\" \"The engagement and powerful skills that people get playing a game — why can't work be like that?\" he asks."}], "readingPassages": {"46": "Junk food is now a staple of many Americans' diets. Advertising campaigns from the snack food companies, often featuring sports stars, send the message that we can neutralize any negative effects of consuming their products simply by getting more physical exercise. But recent studies show a lack of exercise is not to blame for rising obesity rates. Bad diets are.\nInterventions(干预) to help reduce junk food consumption are especially important for children and teenagers. Prevention is better than cure because obesity is so difficult to treat. Unfortunately, while health education has shown some success among young children, teens have been harder to reach.\nNow a large-scale study has tried an innovative approach to change teenagers' attitudes towards healthy eating, and the results are promising. The researchers argued that previous interventions have probably been unsuccessful because of a major flaw: they focused on a future, healthier you and assumed that this would be enough motivation for teenagers. In contrast, the new intervention cleverly exploits teenagers' instinct for rebelliousness and autonomy, and the value they place on social justice.\nTo do this, researchers had students read an article on the food industry. It revealed a secret about the manipulative and deceptive strategies used to make junk food more addictive and characterize the products as healthy. The article also explained how advertising campaigns specifically target very young and poor people, causing harm for these vulnerable groups. Afterwards, the participants read a fictional survey of teens who wanted to \"fight back against the companies by buying and eating less processed food\"\nAfter the intervention, participants associated healthy eating with autonomy and social justice. The teenagers also rated healthy eating as being more appealing. Importantly, there were also some promising effects of the new intervention on actual behavior. A day later the students were offered a choice of snacks and drinks in a seemingly unrelated context. The teens chose healthy snacks and drinks (such as fruit or water) more often over unhealthy options (like biscuits and soda). Apparently associating a healthy diet with teenagers' own values seems to be a promising avenue to prevent obesity.", "47": "Junk food is now a staple of many Americans' diets. Advertising campaigns from the snack food companies, often featuring sports stars, send the message that we can neutralize any negative effects of consuming their products simply by getting more physical exercise. But recent studies show a lack of exercise is not to blame for rising obesity rates. Bad diets are.\nInterventions(干预) to help reduce junk food consumption are especially important for children and teenagers. Prevention is better than cure because obesity is so difficult to treat. Unfortunately, while health education has shown some success among young children, teens have been harder to reach.\nNow a large-scale study has tried an innovative approach to change teenagers' attitudes towards healthy eating, and the results are promising. The researchers argued that previous interventions have probably been unsuccessful because of a major flaw: they focused on a future, healthier you and assumed that this would be enough motivation for teenagers. In contrast, the new intervention cleverly exploits teenagers' instinct for rebelliousness and autonomy, and the value they place on social justice.\nTo do this, researchers had students read an article on the food industry. It revealed a secret about the manipulative and deceptive strategies used to make junk food more addictive and characterize the products as healthy. The article also explained how advertising campaigns specifically target very young and poor people, causing harm for these vulnerable groups. Afterwards, the participants read a fictional survey of teens who wanted to \"fight back against the companies by buying and eating less processed food\"\nAfter the intervention, participants associated healthy eating with autonomy and social justice. The teenagers also rated healthy eating as being more appealing. Importantly, there were also some promising effects of the new intervention on actual behavior. A day later the students were offered a choice of snacks and drinks in a seemingly unrelated context. The teens chose healthy snacks and drinks (such as fruit or water) more often over unhealthy options (like biscuits and soda). Apparently associating a healthy diet with teenagers' own values seems to be a promising avenue to prevent obesity.", "48": "Junk food is now a staple of many Americans' diets. Advertising campaigns from the snack food companies, often featuring sports stars, send the message that we can neutralize any negative effects of consuming their products simply by getting more physical exercise. But recent studies show a lack of exercise is not to blame for rising obesity rates. Bad diets are.\nInterventions(干预) to help reduce junk food consumption are especially important for children and teenagers. Prevention is better than cure because obesity is so difficult to treat. Unfortunately, while health education has shown some success among young children, teens have been harder to reach.\nNow a large-scale study has tried an innovative approach to change teenagers' attitudes towards healthy eating, and the results are promising. The researchers argued that previous interventions have probably been unsuccessful because of a major flaw: they focused on a future, healthier you and assumed that this would be enough motivation for teenagers. In contrast, the new intervention cleverly exploits teenagers' instinct for rebelliousness and autonomy, and the value they place on social justice.\nTo do this, researchers had students read an article on the food industry. It revealed a secret about the manipulative and deceptive strategies used to make junk food more addictive and characterize the products as healthy. The article also explained how advertising campaigns specifically target very young and poor people, causing harm for these vulnerable groups. Afterwards, the participants read a fictional survey of teens who wanted to \"fight back against the companies by buying and eating less processed food\"\nAfter the intervention, participants associated healthy eating with autonomy and social justice. The teenagers also rated healthy eating as being more appealing. Importantly, there were also some promising effects of the new intervention on actual behavior. A day later the students were offered a choice of snacks and drinks in a seemingly unrelated context. The teens chose healthy snacks and drinks (such as fruit or water) more often over unhealthy options (like biscuits and soda). Apparently associating a healthy diet with teenagers' own values seems to be a promising avenue to prevent obesity.", "49": "Junk food is now a staple of many Americans' diets. Advertising campaigns from the snack food companies, often featuring sports stars, send the message that we can neutralize any negative effects of consuming their products simply by getting more physical exercise. But recent studies show a lack of exercise is not to blame for rising obesity rates. Bad diets are.\nInterventions(干预) to help reduce junk food consumption are especially important for children and teenagers. Prevention is better than cure because obesity is so difficult to treat. Unfortunately, while health education has shown some success among young children, teens have been harder to reach.\nNow a large-scale study has tried an innovative approach to change teenagers' attitudes towards healthy eating, and the results are promising. The researchers argued that previous interventions have probably been unsuccessful because of a major flaw: they focused on a future, healthier you and assumed that this would be enough motivation for teenagers. In contrast, the new intervention cleverly exploits teenagers' instinct for rebelliousness and autonomy, and the value they place on social justice.\nTo do this, researchers had students read an article on the food industry. It revealed a secret about the manipulative and deceptive strategies used to make junk food more addictive and characterize the products as healthy. The article also explained how advertising campaigns specifically target very young and poor people, causing harm for these vulnerable groups. Afterwards, the participants read a fictional survey of teens who wanted to \"fight back against the companies by buying and eating less processed food\"\nAfter the intervention, participants associated healthy eating with autonomy and social justice. The teenagers also rated healthy eating as being more appealing. Importantly, there were also some promising effects of the new intervention on actual behavior. A day later the students were offered a choice of snacks and drinks in a seemingly unrelated context. The teens chose healthy snacks and drinks (such as fruit or water) more often over unhealthy options (like biscuits and soda). Apparently associating a healthy diet with teenagers' own values seems to be a promising avenue to prevent obesity.", "50": "Junk food is now a staple of many Americans' diets. Advertising campaigns from the snack food companies, often featuring sports stars, send the message that we can neutralize any negative effects of consuming their products simply by getting more physical exercise. But recent studies show a lack of exercise is not to blame for rising obesity rates. Bad diets are.\nInterventions(干预) to help reduce junk food consumption are especially important for children and teenagers. Prevention is better than cure because obesity is so difficult to treat. Unfortunately, while health education has shown some success among young children, teens have been harder to reach.\nNow a large-scale study has tried an innovative approach to change teenagers' attitudes towards healthy eating, and the results are promising. The researchers argued that previous interventions have probably been unsuccessful because of a major flaw: they focused on a future, healthier you and assumed that this would be enough motivation for teenagers. In contrast, the new intervention cleverly exploits teenagers' instinct for rebelliousness and autonomy, and the value they place on social justice.\nTo do this, researchers had students read an article on the food industry. It revealed a secret about the manipulative and deceptive strategies used to make junk food more addictive and characterize the products as healthy. The article also explained how advertising campaigns specifically target very young and poor people, causing harm for these vulnerable groups. Afterwards, the participants read a fictional survey of teens who wanted to \"fight back against the companies by buying and eating less processed food\"\nAfter the intervention, participants associated healthy eating with autonomy and social justice. The teenagers also rated healthy eating as being more appealing. Importantly, there were also some promising effects of the new intervention on actual behavior. A day later the students were offered a choice of snacks and drinks in a seemingly unrelated context. The teens chose healthy snacks and drinks (such as fruit or water) more often over unhealthy options (like biscuits and soda). Apparently associating a healthy diet with teenagers' own values seems to be a promising avenue to prevent obesity.", "51": "Adults dream during REM (rapid eye movement) sleep and infants have loads of REM. So, it might be fair to assume that babies have tons of dreams.\nBut scientists believe REM serves a completely different purpose for newborns and infants than dreaming. When babies are in REM, it allows their brain to develop pathways, connections, and eventually, learn languages.\nSince your baby doesn't dream at this stage of brain development, it's safe to assume babies do not have nightmares, either. Nightmares stem from exposure to trauma(创伤), an overactive imagination, and the normal stresses of everyday life.\nAre you wondering, what do babies dream about? Good question, but the answer is nothing! So, when do babies start dreaming? The general consensus is that they start dreaming around the age of two. Psychologist David Foulkes studies children (from very small kids to teens) to bring the secrets of their dreams to the light of day. In his lab, he lets kids fall asleep and then wakes them 3 times a night and asks them to describe what they recall.\nFoulkes' findings are unsurprising. Basically, little kids have little dreams. But exactly what kids see while dreaming depends on their age. As children develop and grow, their dreams do too.\nDreams of very small kids are usually just snapshots(快照), looking much more like a slideshow than a movie, when compared to the dreams of adults. They heavily feature animals and other familiar sights, like images of people eating. According to Foulkes, \"Children's dream life…seems to be similar to their waking imagination and narration.\" Kids ages 5 – 9 begin seeing moving images and characters in action. Dreams now include multiple events strung together, one after the other. They also start developing greater ability to remember dreams. Still, that's not always the case: When roused during REM sleep, 25% of the kids in Foulkes' studies had no recollection of dreaming, a trend that continues through age 9.\nSo, if your baby seems to be in a stressful state while sleeping or is upset upon waking, there may be other factors at play.", "52": "Adults dream during REM (rapid eye movement) sleep and infants have loads of REM. So, it might be fair to assume that babies have tons of dreams.\nBut scientists believe REM serves a completely different purpose for newborns and infants than dreaming. When babies are in REM, it allows their brain to develop pathways, connections, and eventually, learn languages.\nSince your baby doesn't dream at this stage of brain development, it's safe to assume babies do not have nightmares, either. Nightmares stem from exposure to trauma(创伤), an overactive imagination, and the normal stresses of everyday life.\nAre you wondering, what do babies dream about? Good question, but the answer is nothing! So, when do babies start dreaming? The general consensus is that they start dreaming around the age of two. Psychologist David Foulkes studies children (from very small kids to teens) to bring the secrets of their dreams to the light of day. In his lab, he lets kids fall asleep and then wakes them 3 times a night and asks them to describe what they recall.\nFoulkes' findings are unsurprising. Basically, little kids have little dreams. But exactly what kids see while dreaming depends on their age. As children develop and grow, their dreams do too.\nDreams of very small kids are usually just snapshots(快照), looking much more like a slideshow than a movie, when compared to the dreams of adults. They heavily feature animals and other familiar sights, like images of people eating. According to Foulkes, \"Children's dream life…seems to be similar to their waking imagination and narration.\" Kids ages 5 – 9 begin seeing moving images and characters in action. Dreams now include multiple events strung together, one after the other. They also start developing greater ability to remember dreams. Still, that's not always the case: When roused during REM sleep, 25% of the kids in Foulkes' studies had no recollection of dreaming, a trend that continues through age 9.\nSo, if your baby seems to be in a stressful state while sleeping or is upset upon waking, there may be other factors at play.", "53": "Adults dream during REM (rapid eye movement) sleep and infants have loads of REM. So, it might be fair to assume that babies have tons of dreams.\nBut scientists believe REM serves a completely different purpose for newborns and infants than dreaming. When babies are in REM, it allows their brain to develop pathways, connections, and eventually, learn languages.\nSince your baby doesn't dream at this stage of brain development, it's safe to assume babies do not have nightmares, either. Nightmares stem from exposure to trauma(创伤), an overactive imagination, and the normal stresses of everyday life.\nAre you wondering, what do babies dream about? Good question, but the answer is nothing! So, when do babies start dreaming? The general consensus is that they start dreaming around the age of two. Psychologist David Foulkes studies children (from very small kids to teens) to bring the secrets of their dreams to the light of day. In his lab, he lets kids fall asleep and then wakes them 3 times a night and asks them to describe what they recall.\nFoulkes' findings are unsurprising. Basically, little kids have little dreams. But exactly what kids see while dreaming depends on their age. As children develop and grow, their dreams do too.\nDreams of very small kids are usually just snapshots(快照), looking much more like a slideshow than a movie, when compared to the dreams of adults. They heavily feature animals and other familiar sights, like images of people eating. According to Foulkes, \"Children's dream life…seems to be similar to their waking imagination and narration.\" Kids ages 5 – 9 begin seeing moving images and characters in action. Dreams now include multiple events strung together, one after the other. They also start developing greater ability to remember dreams. Still, that's not always the case: When roused during REM sleep, 25% of the kids in Foulkes' studies had no recollection of dreaming, a trend that continues through age 9.\nSo, if your baby seems to be in a stressful state while sleeping or is upset upon waking, there may be other factors at play.", "54": "Adults dream during REM (rapid eye movement) sleep and infants have loads of REM. So, it might be fair to assume that babies have tons of dreams.\nBut scientists believe REM serves a completely different purpose for newborns and infants than dreaming. When babies are in REM, it allows their brain to develop pathways, connections, and eventually, learn languages.\nSince your baby doesn't dream at this stage of brain development, it's safe to assume babies do not have nightmares, either. Nightmares stem from exposure to trauma(创伤), an overactive imagination, and the normal stresses of everyday life.\nAre you wondering, what do babies dream about? Good question, but the answer is nothing! So, when do babies start dreaming? The general consensus is that they start dreaming around the age of two. Psychologist David Foulkes studies children (from very small kids to teens) to bring the secrets of their dreams to the light of day. In his lab, he lets kids fall asleep and then wakes them 3 times a night and asks them to describe what they recall.\nFoulkes' findings are unsurprising. Basically, little kids have little dreams. But exactly what kids see while dreaming depends on their age. As children develop and grow, their dreams do too.\nDreams of very small kids are usually just snapshots(快照), looking much more like a slideshow than a movie, when compared to the dreams of adults. They heavily feature animals and other familiar sights, like images of people eating. According to Foulkes, \"Children's dream life…seems to be similar to their waking imagination and narration.\" Kids ages 5 – 9 begin seeing moving images and characters in action. Dreams now include multiple events strung together, one after the other. They also start developing greater ability to remember dreams. Still, that's not always the case: When roused during REM sleep, 25% of the kids in Foulkes' studies had no recollection of dreaming, a trend that continues through age 9.\nSo, if your baby seems to be in a stressful state while sleeping or is upset upon waking, there may be other factors at play.", "55": "Adults dream during REM (rapid eye movement) sleep and infants have loads of REM. So, it might be fair to assume that babies have tons of dreams.\nBut scientists believe REM serves a completely different purpose for newborns and infants than dreaming. When babies are in REM, it allows their brain to develop pathways, connections, and eventually, learn languages.\nSince your baby doesn't dream at this stage of brain development, it's safe to assume babies do not have nightmares, either. Nightmares stem from exposure to trauma(创伤), an overactive imagination, and the normal stresses of everyday life.\nAre you wondering, what do babies dream about? Good question, but the answer is nothing! So, when do babies start dreaming? The general consensus is that they start dreaming around the age of two. Psychologist David Foulkes studies children (from very small kids to teens) to bring the secrets of their dreams to the light of day. In his lab, he lets kids fall asleep and then wakes them 3 times a night and asks them to describe what they recall.\nFoulkes' findings are unsurprising. Basically, little kids have little dreams. But exactly what kids see while dreaming depends on their age. As children develop and grow, their dreams do too.\nDreams of very small kids are usually just snapshots(快照), looking much more like a slideshow than a movie, when compared to the dreams of adults. They heavily feature animals and other familiar sights, like images of people eating. According to Foulkes, \"Children's dream life…seems to be similar to their waking imagination and narration.\" Kids ages 5 – 9 begin seeing moving images and characters in action. Dreams now include multiple events strung together, one after the other. They also start developing greater ability to remember dreams. Still, that's not always the case: When roused during REM sleep, 25% of the kids in Foulkes' studies had no recollection of dreaming, a trend that continues through age 9.\nSo, if your baby seems to be in a stressful state while sleeping or is upset upon waking, there may be other factors at play."}, "questions": [{"id": "2025-12-2-l-1", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 1, "stem": "What do we learn from the news report about the egg price in the U.S.?", "options": ["A) It has gone up sharply.", "B) It has been fluctuating.", "C) It has risen because of bird flu.", "D) It has been consumers' concern."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题） It has gone up sharply.\n【定位】The U.S. average price for a dozen large eggs last month hit $4.25. That's more than double\ntheir $1.78 price in December, 2023.\n上个月美国一打大号鸡蛋的平均价格达到了 4.25 美元，这比 2023 年 12 月的 1.78 美元还\n高出一倍多。\n【信号】数字对比+more than double。答案句的信号是数字加比较：hit $4.25 与 more than\ndouble their $1.78 price——从 1.78 涨到 4.25、还『翻了不止一倍』，是明确的大幅上\n涨。新闻里出现两个价格数字相比较，往往就是答案句。\n【替换】more than double their $1.78 price ↔ gone up sharply\n倍数=大幅\n【排除】B) 未提及\nIt has been fluctuating. 说价格『来回波动』，但录音只给了从 1.78 到 4.25 的单向上\n涨，没有任何下跌或反复，方向不符。\nC) 原词陷阱\nIt has risen because of bird flu. 把上涨原因归给禽流感。录音确实念到 bird flu，但那是\n解释 The Department of Agriculture banned eggs from Mexico（为何禁止从墨西哥带\n蛋）的原因，不是涨价的原因——盗用了原词却接错了因果。\n借用原文：This was due to the risk of bird flu.\nD) 未提及\nIt has been consumers' concern. 说蛋价成了消费者的担忧。录音通篇没提消费者的态度\n或担忧，只客观报了价格数字，属无中生有。", "points": ["听力·长对话", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2025-12-2-l-2", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 2, "stem": "What does the news report say about most people arriving at American borders with eggs?", "options": ["A) They don't want to abandon their eggs.", "B) They don't know they will be heavily fined.", "C) They don't know they are forbidden to bring eggs.", "D) They don't declare their eggs during the inspection."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题） They don't know they are forbidden to bring eggs.\n【定位】Officials said most people arriving at international borders with eggs are truthful about their\npurchases. They simply don't know bringing eggs is not allowed.\n官员说，多数带着鸡蛋到达国际边境的人对自己买的东西是如实交代的。他们只是不知道带\n鸡蛋是不被允许的。\n【信号】题干同词组+紧跟解释句。答案句信号是题干原词组在录音里复现：most people arriving\nat international borders with eggs 几乎照搬了题干，它后面紧跟的 They simply don't\nknow bringing eggs is not allowed 就是对这群人的正面交代，正是答案落点。\n【替换】They simply don't know bringing eggs is not allowed ↔\nThey don't know they are forbidden to bring eggs.\nnot allowed=forbidden\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\nThey don't want to abandon their eggs. 说他们不愿放弃鸡蛋。录音里确有 abandon\nthe eggs 这个词，但原文是说『在第一次检查时申报、然后可以无后果地放弃鸡蛋』，根\n本没说他们『不愿放弃』——盗用 abandon 一词，意思却恰相反。\n借用原文：Then the person can abandon the eggs without consequence\nB) 偷换概念\nThey don't know they will be heavily fined. 说他们不知会被重罚。录音里 risk fines of\nup to $10,000、a $300 penalty 确实提到罚款，但原文说这群人不知道的是『带蛋被禁\n止』，不是『不知会被罚款』——把『不知禁令』偷换成了『不知罚款』。\n借用原文：Those bringing eggs into the U.S. risk fines of up to $10,000.\nD) 与原文相反\nThey don't declare their eggs during the inspection. 说他们检查时不申报。录音里\ndeclared during the first inspection 确实有 declare 和 inspection，但原文是说『物品\n一般会在第一次检查时被申报』，即多数人是申报的，与选项的『不申报』正好相反。\n借用原文：Generally, the items are declared during the first inspection.\nQuestions 3 and 4 are based on the news report you have just heard.\n1 Nettie's House, a restaurant in America, has been hit with criticism after announcing on the website\nthat children under the age of 10 are no longer welcome.\n美国一家名为Nettie's House的餐厅在其网站上宣布不再欢迎10岁以下儿童后遭到批评。\n2 Among the reasons given for the decision, the restaurant cited \"noise levels, lack of space for high\nchairs, cleaning up crazy messes, and the liability of kids running around the restaurant\". They decided\nthat it was time to take control. The decision wasn't made lightly, but some recent events pushed them to\nimplement this new policy.\n餐厅给出的决定理由包括\"噪音水平、高脚椅空间不足、清理混乱不堪的场面以及孩子在餐厅跑来跑去的\n责任问题\"。他们决定是时候采取管控措施了。这个决定并非轻率做出,但最近发生的一些事件促使他们实\n施这项新政策。\n3 Responding to the post, one person commented that \"It is messed up beyond another level. Children\nare children. Mums are tired and don't have time to cook. Not welcoming children under 10 is a\ncomplete slap in the face to families.\" He felt so disappointed in the establishment.\n针对这篇帖子,有人评论说\"这简直乱得不成样子。孩子就是孩子。妈妈们很累,没有时间做饭。不欢迎10岁\n以下儿童完全是对家庭的一记耳光。\"他对这家餐厅感到非常失望。\n4 There are others, however, who showed support for the restaurant's decision. They said it has the right\nto implement any rules it wants.\n然而,也有其他人对餐厅的决定表示支持。他们说餐厅有权实施任何它想要的规定。", "points": ["听力·长对话", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2025-12-2-l-3", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 3, "stem": "What do we learn from the news report about the restaurant Nettie's House?", "options": ["A) It is no longer critical of younger customers.", "B) It has banned kids under 10 from dining there.", "C) It is no longer as noisy and crowded as before.", "D) It has implemented a decision to expand business."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题） It has banned kids under 10 from dining there.\n【定位】Nettie's House, a restaurant in America, has been hit with criticism after announcing on the\nwebsite that children under the age of 10 are no longer welcome.\n美国一家名叫 Nettie's House 的餐厅在网站上宣布不再欢迎 10 岁以下儿童后，遭到了\n批评。\n【信号】新闻首句点题，机构名后紧跟核心事件。短篇新闻第一句通常就把核心事件说清。这里店名\n之后紧跟 announcing on the website that children under the age of 10 are no longer\nwelcome，这正是\"该餐厅做了什么\"的答案句，顺着开头第一句就能抓到。\n【替换】children under the age of 10 are no longer welcome ↔ banned kids under 10 from dining there\n\"不再欢迎\"=\"禁止用餐\"的同义改写\n【排除】A) 与原文相反\nIt is no longer critical of younger customers. 借了原文\"针对低龄顾客\"这层意思，却说餐\n厅\"不再挑剔年轻顾客\"——恰恰相反，餐厅是收紧了对儿童的限制；而且 no longer\ncritical 的是公众对餐厅的批评，不是餐厅对顾客的态度，方向反了。\n借用原文：children under the age of 10 are no longer welcome\nC) 原词陷阱\nIt is no longer as noisy and crowded as before. 抓住了原文提到的 noise levels（吵闹）\n来勾人，但原文只是把吵闹列为出台新规的\"理由\"，从没说餐厅现在已经变得不吵不挤了。\n听到 noise 就选 C 就中了原词陷阱。\n借用原文：noise levels\nD) 原词陷阱\nIt has implemented a decision to expand business. 蹭了原文里 implement this new\npolicy 的 implement 一词，但原文实施的是\"谢绝儿童\"的新政策，根本没提扩张生意，\nexpand business 是无中生有。\n借用原文：implement this new policy", "points": ["听力·长对话", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2025-12-2-l-4", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 4, "stem": "What do supporters say in response to the restaurant's new policy?", "options": ["A) It is fully aware of frequent customers' feelings.", "B) It caters to the tastes of different customers.", "C) It is entitled to adopt any rules it wants.", "D) It should announce its decisions online."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （态度观点题） It is entitled to adopt any rules it wants.\n【定位】There are others, however, who showed support for the restaurant's decision. They said it has\nthe right to implement any rules it wants.\n不过也有另一些人对餐厅的决定表示支持。他们说餐厅有权实施它想要的任何规定。\n【信号】转折 however 引出支持方，应答句紧随。第4段以 There are others, however, who\nshowed support 用转折词 however 把镜头从前文反对者切到支持者，紧接着 They said\nit has the right to implement any rules it wants 就是支持者的原话——题问\nsupporters，落点正是这句应答。\n【替换】it has the right to implement any rules it wants ↔ It is entitled to adopt any rules it wants\nhas the right=is entitled，implement=adopt\n【排除】A) 未提及\nIt is fully aware of frequent customers' feelings. 说餐厅\"充分了解常客的感受\"，但支持\n者原话只讲\"餐厅有权定规矩\"，从没提常客（frequent customers）也没说店家了解谁的感\n受，这层意思原文整个没出现。\nB) 原词陷阱\nIt caters to the tastes of different customers. 蹭了全篇反复出现的 customers 一词，但\n支持者说的是\"店家有权定规矩\"，不是说它\"迎合不同顾客的口味\"——caters to tastes 在\n原文没有依据，是借熟词编的好听话。\n借用原文：Children are children\nD) 原词陷阱\nIt should announce its decisions online. 蹭了原文 announcing on the website 的\"网上\n宣布\"，但那只是叙述餐厅当初怎么发的公告，支持者根本没说\"店家应该在网上公布决定\"，\n且 should 是建议口吻、与本题问的支持表态不符。\n借用原文：announcing on the website", "points": ["听力·长对话", "态度观点题"]}, {"id": "2025-12-2-l-5", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 5, "stem": "What does the report say security officers were searching for?", "options": ["A) A missing pet.", "B) A grown-up snake.", "C) A huge animal skin.", "D) A snake specialist."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题） A grown-up snake.\n【定位】Security officers were searching a suburb in Australia where an adult snake was believed to\nbe wandering after the discovery of a huge, \"freshly shed\" snake skin.\n安保人员正在搜查澳大利亚的一处郊区，据信有一条成年蛇在那里游荡，此前那里发现了一\n张巨大的、刚蜕下的蛇皮。\n【信号】搜寻主语句直接点对象。答案句是新闻第一句，把『谁在搜+搜什么』一并说清：an adult\nsnake was believed to be wandering（一条成年蛇据信在游荡），这才是被搜寻的活物；\n蛇皮 snake skin 只是引出搜寻的线索，不是搜寻目标。\n【替换】an adult snake ↔ A grown-up snake.\nadult=grown-up 同义改写\n【排除】C) 原词陷阱\n选项盗用了录音里真实出现的 a huge, \"freshly shed\" snake skin（巨大的刚蜕的蛇皮），\n听到 huge 和 skin 很想选 A huge animal skin.。但蛇皮只是引发搜寻的线索，搜寻的对象\n是活蛇本身，故 C 答非所问。\n借用原文：a huge, \"freshly shed\" snake skin\nA) 未提及\n录音通篇说的是一条野生大蛇，从未提到这是谁走失的宠物。A missing pet. 在原文中没有\n依据，属凭常识臆测（『蛇会不会是谁养的跑了』），录音没说就不能选。\nD) 张冠李戴\nA snake specialist.（捕蛇/治蛇专家）盗用了录音后文 a licensed snake catcher 与 a\nspecialist animal doctor 的概念，但这些人是抓到蛇之后才会请的，不是此刻搜寻的对\n象。把『搜寻蛇』偷换成『搜寻专家』，张冠李戴。\n借用原文：a specialist animal doctor", "points": ["听力·长对话", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2025-12-2-l-6", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 6, "stem": "Where does the report say the discovery was made?", "options": ["A) In a government office.", "B) In a deserted house.", "C) At a local zoo.", "D) At a construction site."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题） At a construction site.\n【定位】The skin was found at a construction site, and the government wrote to residents to\nwarn them.\n那张蛇皮是在一处建筑工地发现的，政府随后致信居民提醒他们。\n【信号】地点状语 at 直接给地点。答案句里 The skin was found at a construction site 的 at a\nconstruction site 是典型的地点状语，正面回答了『发现在哪』。问 where 就抓这种 at/in\n+ 场所的结构。\n【替换】found at a construction site ↔ At a construction site.\n地点词原样对应\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\n选项盗用了同句里的 the government 一词，听到 government 就想选 In a government\noffice.。但录音里政府是『写信提醒居民』的施动方，并非发现蛇皮的地点，二者无关，属\n答非所问。\n借用原文：the government wrote to residents\nB) 未提及\nIn a deserted house.（废弃房屋）录音从未提到，可能是考生凭『蛇出没=破旧荒地』的\n常识脑补出来的。原文只说工地，没说废屋，无依据。\nC) 未提及\nAt a local zoo.（本地动物园）也是常识联想（『蛇=动物=动物园』），录音里完全没有出\n现 zoo。原文发现地是工地，不是动物园，排除。", "points": ["听力·长对话", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2025-12-2-l-7", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 7, "stem": "What did the government say would be done if security officers found what they were searching for?", "options": ["A) Figuring out where it came from.", "B) Curing it of any possible diseases.", "C) Protecting its babies against harm.", "D) Determining how to catch it safely."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题） Figuring out where it came from.\n【定位】If caught, the snake would be examined to determine where it came from, how long it had\nbeen in the area, what it had been eating, whether it was carrying any diseases of concern and\nwhether it had produced babies.\n一旦抓获，这条蛇将接受检查，以确定它从哪里来、在该区域待了多久、一直在吃什么、是\n否携带值得关注的疾病，以及是否已经产下幼蛇。\n【信号】If caught 引出后续措施。答案句以 If caught（一旦抓获）开头，正对题干的 if…found 假\n设条件，后面 would be examined to determine where it came from 列出抓到后要做的\n事，是本题答案落点。\n【替换】examined to determine where it came from ↔ Figuring out where it came from.\ndetermine=figure out 同义\n【排除】B) 偷换概念\n选项盗用了录音 whether it was carrying any diseases of concern 里的 diseases 一词，\n听到『病』就想选 Curing it of any possible diseases.。但原文只是『检查它是否携带疾\n病』，并非『治好它的病』，把『检查』偷换成『治疗』，故错。\n借用原文：whether it was carrying any diseases of concern\nC) 偷换概念\n选项盗用了 whether it had produced babies 里的 babies，让人误选 Protecting its\nbabies against harm.。但原文是『查清它是否产过幼蛇』，根本不是『保护幼蛇不受伤\n害』，方向完全不同，属偷换概念。\n借用原文：whether it had produced babies\nD) 原词陷阱\nDetermining how to catch it safely. 盗用了 catch（抓）。录音里抓蛇是 employ a\nlicensed snake catcher to catch it（雇持证捕蛇人去抓），是直接安排人去抓，并不是\n『研究怎样安全地抓』，故 D 答非所问。\n借用原文：employ a licensed snake catcher to catch it", "points": ["听力·长对话", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2025-12-2-l-8", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 8, "stem": "Why does the woman say she swears it?", "options": ["A) The man doesn't agree Hannah is poor.", "B) The man doesn't believe what she says.", "C) The man doesn't think she can be a millionaire.", "D) The man doesn't consider her to be trustworthy."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （推断题） The man doesn't believe what she says.\n【定位】M: I can't believe it. You're really not joking, are you?\n男：我真不敢相信。你真的不是在开玩笑吧？\n【信号】发誓是对男士『不信』的回应。女士反复发誓的触发点，是男士这句 I can't believe it.\nYou're really not joking, are you?——男士先表态不信、追问是否开玩笑，女士才被迫发\n誓。这句男士的怀疑就是答案信号，前面 No way. That's incredible. Are you kidding? 也\n铺垫了同样的不信。\n【替换】I can't believe it. ↔ doesn't believe what she says\nI can't believe=doesn't believe\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\n选项 The man doesn't agree Hannah is poor. 借了录音开头 Poor Hannah! 的 poor 来勾\n人，但男士从没就『Hannah 穷不穷』表过态，他不信的是『中了大奖』这件事，与穷不穷\n无关。\n借用原文：Poor Hannah!\nC) 偷换概念\n选项 The man doesn't think she can be a millionaire. 抓了录音 became a millionaire\novernight 里的 millionaire，但男士震惊的是『这事是不是真的』，并非质疑『她有没有能\n力成为富翁』——把『不信事实』偷换成了『不信她的能力』。\n借用原文：became a millionaire overnight\nD) 过度推断\n选项 The man doesn't consider her to be trustworthy. 听着和『不信』近似，但\ntrustworthy 指『这个人不可信、人品有问题』，男士只是惊讶于消息太离奇、问对方是否\n开玩笑，并没有否定女士的人品，属过度引申。", "points": ["听力·长对话", "推断题"]}, {"id": "2025-12-2-l-9", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 9, "stem": "What do we learn about Hannah from the conversation?", "options": ["A) She no longer has any money left.", "B) She has been betrayed by her family.", "C) She can no longer hold on to her job.", "D) She has been robbed of 5 million Euros."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题） She no longer has any money left.\n【定位】W: Well, it turns out that she's spent it all already.\n女：嗯，原来她已经把钱全花光了。\n【信号】转折后交代真实结局。前文一直在讲 Hannah 中了大奖、好运连连，直到女士这句 it turns\nout that she's spent it all already 才点出真相——钱花光了。it turns out(原来)是语气转\n折信号，转折之后才是这道题要的事实。she ran out of money 再次确认了这一点。\n【替换】she's spent it all already ↔ no longer has any money left\n全花光=不再有钱\n【排除】B) 未提及\n选项 She has been betrayed by her family. 在录音里找不到任何依据——对话提到她给家\n人买房，是付出而非被背叛，『被家人背叛』纯属编造。\nC) 与原文相反\n选项 She can no longer hold on to her job. 抓了录音 quit the job she hates 里的 job，\n但录音是说她原想辞掉讨厌的工作却辞不成、只能继续干，恰恰是『保得住/必须保住』工\n作，选项却说成『保不住』，把意思反了。\n借用原文：she was planning to quit the job she hates and retire early. But now that she ran out of\nmoney, she can't\nD) 原词陷阱\n选项 She has been robbed of 5 million Euros. 用了录音里真念到的钱数 maybe five\nmillion Euros after tax 来制造熟悉感，但钱是她自己花光的(买房买车)，不是被人抢走，\nrobbed 完全没有依据。\n借用原文：maybe five million Euros after tax", "points": ["听力·篇章", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2025-12-2-l-10", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 10, "stem": "What does the man think really matters to people?", "options": ["A) Fortune.", "B) Family.", "C) Harmony.", "D) Happiness."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题） Happiness.\n【定位】M: Wow. Are they all happy? Because that's what matters, isn't it?\n男：哇，他们都开心吗？因为那才是最要紧的，不是吗？\n【信号】matters 原词点名 + 反问强调。男士直接说 that's what matters, isn't it?，其中 matters\n与题干 really matters 对得上；而 that 指的正是前半句的 Are they all happy?——所以他\n认为最要紧的是『开心/幸福』。反问句 isn't it 是加重语气的强调信号。\n【替换】Are they all happy? ↔ Happiness.\nhappy→Happiness 形转名\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\n选项 Fortune. 和整段『中大奖、五百万』的话题最贴，最易顺手误选，但通篇没有任何人\n说『财富才是最要紧的』，男士点名的是开心，不是钱。\n借用原文：maybe five million Euros after tax\nB) 原词陷阱\n选项 Family. 借了录音里反复出现的家人(给 her parents、四个兄弟姐妹买房)，但男士说\n『要紧』时指的是大家开不开心，不是『家庭』这个概念本身。\n借用原文：a house for her parents, and then a house for each of her four brothers and sisters\nC) 未提及\n选项 Harmony.(和睦)看似和『家人开心』沾点边，但录音里既没念到 harmony 这个词，\n也没人表达『和睦才最重要』，纯属凭话题臆造。", "points": ["听力·篇章", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2025-12-2-l-11", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 11, "stem": "What does Hannah's husband say he'll do if she sells the new car?", "options": ["A) Divorce her.", "B) Leave home.", "C) Buy her a more expensive model.", "D) Let the best thing happen to them."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题） Divorce her.\n【定位】W: She wants to, but her husband refuses to get rid of it. He says the car is the best thing that\nhas ever happened to him, and if she sells it, he will divorce her.\n女：她想卖，但她丈夫拒绝处理掉它。他说这辆车是他这辈子遇到的最好的东西，如果她卖\n了，他就跟她离婚。\n【信号】if…he will… 条件句直给答案。答案句就是 if she sells it, he will divorce her——题干的\n条件 if she sells 在这里原样出现，紧跟的主句 he will divorce her 就是丈夫扬言要做的\n事。if 引导的条件主句是这道题的答案信号。\n【替换】he will divorce her ↔ Divorce her.\n原句动作直接照搬\n【排除】B) 偷换概念\n选项 Leave home. 听着和『离婚』情绪相近，但录音里丈夫说的是 divorce(离婚)，从没提\n『离家出走』，属凭联想替换。\nC) 原词陷阱\n选项 Buy her a more expensive model. 抓了录音 a ridiculously expensive sports car\n的 expensive，但录音里丈夫是『拒绝卖掉现有的车』，根本不是要再买更贵的，方向相\n反。\n借用原文：a ridiculously expensive sports car\nD) 原词陷阱\n选项 Let the best thing happen to them. 改自录音 the car is the best thing that has\never happened to him，但那是丈夫形容车好、是已经发生的事，并非他在『卖车后要做\n的动作』，把一句描述硬塞成了答案。\n借用原文：the car is the best thing that has ever happened to him", "points": ["听力·篇章", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2025-12-2-l-12", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 12, "stem": "What does the man say about Jake?", "options": ["A) He is not outstanding in Science and Math.", "B) He does not have a strong academic record.", "C) He is not yet mature enough to live on his own.", "D) He does not like Westwood Elementary School."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题） He is not yet mature enough to live on his own.\n【定位】I admit that their academic record is very strong with many outstanding awards for\nachievements in Science and Math, but he is just not mature enough to live on his own at\nthis point.\n我承认他们的学术成绩很强，在科学和数学方面拿过很多杰出的奖项，但他现在还不够成\n熟，没法独立生活。\n【信号】转折 but 之后才是男方真意。这句前半是 I admit…让步夸学校，真正的态度落在 but he is\njust not mature enough to live on his own at this point 之后。听力里 but 之后才是落\n点，前半的赞扬是铺垫，不能当答案。\n【替换】he is just not mature enough to live on his own ↔\nHe is not yet mature enough to live on his own.\n几乎原句，just→yet 同义\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\n选项说 not outstanding in Science and Math，而录音 outstanding awards for\nachievements in Science and Math 恰恰是说学校在科学数学上很突出。它盗用了原词\nScience and Math，却把\"突出\"反说成\"不突出\"。\n借用原文：outstanding awards for achievements in Science and Math\nB) 偷换概念\n录音明说 their academic record is very strong，指的还是学校的学术记录强；选项 He\ndoes not have a strong academic record. 既把对象从学校换成 Jake，又把\"强\"反成\"不\n强\"。\n借用原文：their academic record is very strong\nD) 未提及\nWestwood 是女方想送的 boarding school，男方的顾虑是孩子\"不够成熟\"，全程没说\nJake 喜不喜欢这所学校，He does not like Westwood Elementary School. 属凭空添加。", "points": ["听力·篇章", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2025-12-2-l-13", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 13, "stem": "Why does the woman feel uncomfortable about sending Jake to Lakeview School?", "options": ["A) It is too far away.", "B) It is newly opened.", "C) It lacks outdoor sports facilities.", "D) It has too few first-class teachers."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题） It is newly opened.\n【定位】They did just open, though, so I don't think I feel comfortable having our son in the first class\nof a brand-new school.\n不过他们才刚开学，所以我不太放心让我们的儿子去一所全新学校的第一届。\n【信号】though 转折 + 不安的直接陈述。前文女方先夸 Lakeview 校园好、空气好，though 一转\n才道出顾虑，紧接 I don't think I feel comfortable having our son in the first class of a\nbrand-new school，与题干 feel uncomfortable 直接呼应，是答案句。\n【替换】They did just open ↔ It is newly opened.\ndid just open = newly opened\nthe first class of a brand-new school ↔ It is newly opened.\nbrand-new school 印证\"新开\"\n【排除】A) 张冠李戴\nIt is too far away. 谈的是距离，但女方对 Lakeview 的顾虑是\"太新\"，文中没说 Lakeview\n远；\"近\"反而是后面本地学校的优点。距离话题被张冠李戴到了 Lakeview 头上。\n借用原文：Being practically next door\nC) 与原文相反\n录音说 Lakeview a great outdoor campus where he could finally learn all those\noutdoor sports，户外运动是它的优点；选项 It lacks outdoor sports facilities. 把\"有大\n操场练户外运动\"反说成\"缺户外运动设施\"。\n借用原文：a great outdoor campus where he could finally learn all those outdoor sports\nD) 未提及\nIt has too few first-class teachers. 谈师资，但整段讲 Lakeview 只提了校园、空气、刚开\n学，从没提教师数量或质量，属凭空添加。", "points": ["听力·篇章", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2025-12-2-l-14", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 14, "stem": "What does the man say about their local school?", "options": ["A) It has a beautiful campus.", "B) It is within walking distance.", "C) It has a wonderful arts program.", "D) It is helpful to Jake's breathing problems."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题） It has a wonderful arts program.\n【定位】What about our local school then? They have a wonderful arts program that would really\nchallenge Jake in terms of creativity and imagination.\n那我们本地的学校呢？他们有一个很棒的艺术项目，能在创造力和想象力方面真正挑\n战 Jake。\n【信号】男方主动提议本校时主推的卖点。男方用 What about our local school then? 把话题转\n到本地学校，紧接给出的第一条理由 a wonderful arts program 就是他主推的卖点，是答\n案句。\n【替换】a wonderful arts program ↔ It has a wonderful arts program.\n几乎原句复现\n【排除】A) 张冠李戴\nIt has a beautiful campus. 谈校园，但漂亮校园说的是 Lakeview（a great outdoor\ncampus、so much greenery and fresh air），不是本地学校，属张冠李戴。\n借用原文：a great outdoor campus\nB) 原词陷阱\n本地学校确实近，Being practically next door，但题干问 man 关于本校\"说了什么\"，他\n主推的是艺术项目；It is within walking distance. 虽是事实细节，却非他强调的卖点，且\n与正解只能取其一时，原文用 wonderful 着重的是 arts program。\n借用原文：Being practically next door\nD) 张冠李戴\nIt is helpful to Jake's breathing problems. 谈呼吸，但能改善呼吸的是 Lakeview 的清新\n空气（his current breathing problems would probably disappear），与本地学校无\n关，属张冠李戴。\n借用原文：his current breathing problems would probably disappear", "points": ["听力·篇章", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2025-12-2-l-15", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 15, "stem": "What does the woman suggest at the end of the conversation?", "options": ["A) Asking Jake which school he himself prefers to enroll in.", "B) Thinking twice about what is best for Jake's education next year.", "C) Consulting her mother about which school Jake is to attend.", "D) Sending Jake to the school near the home of the man's mother."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题） Sending Jake to the school near the home of the man's mother.\n【定位】What if we send him to the place near your mother's house?\n要不我们把他送到你妈妈家附近的那所学校怎么样？\n【信号】建议句式 What if 在结尾出现。对话最后一句 What if we send him to the place near\nyour mother's house? 用 What if 提出新方案，是女方对男方说的，your mother 指男方\n的母亲，正是结尾建议，为答案句。\n【替换】the place near your mother's house ↔\nSending Jake to the school near the home of the man's mother.\nyour mother = the man's mother\nsend him to the place ↔ Sending Jake to the school\nsend him = sending Jake，place=school\n【排除】A) 未提及\nAsking Jake which school he himself prefers to enroll in. 让 Jake 自己选，但结尾女方\n提的是送到男方母亲家附近，全程没说征求 Jake 意愿，属凭空添加。\nB) 答非所问\nThinking twice about what is best for Jake's education next year. 把 what exactly we\nare going to do for Jake's education next year 这句开头的话改写过来，但那是对话开端\n女方提出讨论的引子，不是结尾给出的具体建议。\n借用原文：what exactly we are going to do for Jake's education next year\nC) 张冠李戴\nConsulting her mother about which school Jake is to attend. 盗用结尾的 mother，却\n把人物彻底换了：原文是\"送到你（男方）妈妈家附近的学校\"，选项变成\"去咨询她（女方）\n自己的妈妈\"，既改了是谁的母亲，又把\"送去就读\"换成\"征求意见\"。\n借用原文：the place near your mother's house", "points": ["听力·篇章", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2025-12-2-l-16", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 16, "stem": "What does the passage say is a major flaw of analytical decision-making?", "options": ["A) It is time consuming.", "B) It is unfit for crucial issues.", "C) It relies too much on solid data.", "D) It dulls a leader's sense of urgency."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题） It is time consuming.\n【定位】A key flaw with analytical decision-making is that it takes time.\n分析型决策的一个关键缺陷是它很费时间。\n【信号】关键词 key flaw 直接亮出缺点。录音第 2 段开头 A key flaw with analytical decision-\nmaking is that it takes time 用 A key flaw 这个\"明示缺点\"信号词领出答案句——耳朵一\n抓到 flaw 就该锁定其后内容，这正是预读时守的信号。\n【替换】it takes time ↔ It is time consuming\ntakes time = time consuming 同义改写\n【排除】B) 原词陷阱\n录音说的是 it is problematic when time is crucial——\"当时间紧迫时它才成问题\"，B 却\n把 crucial 搬来说成 It is unfit for crucial issues（不适合关键\"问题\"），把\"时间紧迫\"偷换\n成\"关键议题\"，听到 crucial 就选会中招。\n借用原文：it is problematic when time is crucial\nC) 偷换概念\nIt relies too much on solid data 把\"靠 solid data\"说成缺点，但录音里 makes\nimportant business decisions only with solid data 是在客观描述这种风格、第 4 段更\n把\"靠数据\"算作优点，C 把中性/优点歪曲成缺陷，与题干问的 flaw 不符。\n借用原文：makes important business decisions only with solid data\nD) 过度推断\n录音只说分析型决策者 struggle with deadlines and a sense of urgency（应付不来紧迫\n感），并未说它\"钝化/削弱\"领导的紧迫感；D 的 dulls a leader's sense of urgency 是借\nurgency 一词反向引申，原文无此意。\n借用原文：struggle with deadlines and a sense of urgency", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2025-12-2-l-17", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 17, "stem": "What does a leader tend to do when they are committed to analytical decision-making?", "options": ["A) Depend on digital technology.", "B) Make assumptions and guesses.", "C) Ignore others' thoughts and ideas.", "D) Lay emphasis on thinking flexibly."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题） Ignore others' thoughts and ideas.\n【定位】Inflexibility causes you to ignore or avoid listening to the thoughts and ideas of others.\n这种僵化会使你忽视或回避去倾听他人的想法和主意。\n【信号】因果信号 causes 引出结果行为。录音第 3 段 Inflexibility causes you to ignore or avoid\nlistening to the thoughts and ideas of others 用因果词 causes 把\"僵化\"导向具体行\n为\"忽视他人想法\"——因果信号后面的结果句往往就是\"会去做什么\"的答案。\n【替换】ignore or avoid listening to the thoughts and ideas of others ↔ Ignore others' thoughts and ideas\n原句压缩成短语，意思不变\n【排除】A) 张冠李戴\nDepend on digital technology 像是从第 4 段 Database technology enables business\nleaders 摘来的——那是讲\"优点\"的语境，与第 17 题问的\"僵化导致的行为\"不是一回事，\n且原文用的是 database technology 而非泛指的 digital technology。\n借用原文：Database technology enables business leaders\nB) 与原文相反\n录音里 get away from the habit of making guesses 说的是分析型决策者\"摆脱猜测\"，B\n的 Make assumptions and guesses 恰恰相反——这类人不会去做假设猜测，B 取了原词\nguesses 却把极性反了。\n借用原文：get away from the habit of making guesses\nD) 与原文相反\n录音明说 hinders the manager's flexibility、Inflexibility——这类领导是\"妨碍灵活、僵\n化\"，D 的 Lay emphasis on thinking flexibly 把它说成\"强调灵活思考\"，与原文意思正好\n相反。\n借用原文：hinders the manager's flexibility", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2025-12-2-l-18", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 18, "stem": "What is a key strength of analytical decision-making?", "options": ["A) Maximizing the advantage of available resources.", "B) Minimizing the possibility of a wrong decision.", "C) Discovering new potential markets.", "D) Perfecting promotional strategies."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题） Minimizing the possibility of a wrong decision.\n【定位】By relying on facts and data, you minimize your potential for a wrong decision.\n通过依靠事实和数据，你把做出错误决策的可能性降到最低。\n【信号】段首 strengths 转好 + minimize 点优点。第 4 段以 does have several key strengths\n由弊转利，紧接 you minimize your potential for a wrong decision 给出具体优点\n——\"转好\"信号词 strengths 后面那句就是答案句。\n【替换】minimize your potential for a wrong decision ↔ Minimizing the possibility of a wrong decision\npotential ≈ possibility 近义替换\n【排除】A) 未提及\nMaximizing the advantage of available resources（最大化资源优势）听来很像个优点，\n但录音讲优点处只说 minimize your potential for a wrong decision 和摆脱猜测，从未\n提\"资源优势\"，属凭常识硬安。\nC) 原词陷阱\n录音第 4 段确有 markets：analyzing target markets and promotional strategies，但那\n是说\"用数据分析现有目标市场\"，C 却说成 Discovering new potential markets（发现新\n市场），听到 markets 就选会中招。\n借用原文：analyzing target markets and promotional strategies\nD) 原词陷阱\nD 的 Perfecting promotional strategies 直接搬走录音里的 promotional strategies，但\n原文是说\"用数据去分析促销策略\"，并非\"完善促销策略\"是它的优点，词对了、意思却错\n了。\n借用原文：analyzing target markets and promotional strategies", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2025-12-2-l-19", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 19, "stem": "What do we learn about Branyas Morera?", "options": ["A) She is one of the world's best-known pianists.", "B) She celebrated her 110th birthday this April.", "C) She moved to the U.S. at a young age.", "D) She is the world's oldest individual."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题） She is the world's oldest individual.\n【定位】Branyas Morera is now 115 years and 320 days old. That means she's been alive longer than\nany other living individual.\n布兰亚斯·莫雷拉现在 115 岁零 320 天。这意味着她比任何其他在世的人都活得更久。\n【信号】longer than 任何人即最年长。答案信号是比较结构 longer than any other living\nindividual——“比任何其他在世者都活得久”就等于“世上最年长”，这种“er than any\nother”的极限表述是定位最高级类信息的标志。\n【替换】longer than any other living individual ↔ the world's oldest individual\n比较级换最高级\n【排除】A) 过度推断\n原文确实提到她弹钢琴，但只说弹琴是她长寿秘诀之一，从未说她是“世界知名钢琴家”；\none of the world's best-known pianists把“会弹琴”拔高成“知名钢琴家”，是过度推断。\n借用原文：playing the piano from a young age\nB) 原词陷阱\n原文说她现在 115 years and 320 days old，是 115 岁，本选项却说她四月庆祝“110 岁生\n日”；它盗用了原文真实存在的出生日期“April 4th”的月份与年龄数字来拼凑，数字被偷换，\n听到“April”就选会中招。\n借用原文：115 years and 320 days old\nC) 与原文相反\n原文说她出生在美国，后来 her family moved to Spain——是搬去西班牙；本选项却说她\n“年幼时搬到美国”，把方向彻底说反了。它盗用“moved”“young child”等原词，只把目的地\n颠倒，专坑没听清方向的人。\n借用原文：her family moved to Spain", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2025-12-2-l-20", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 20, "stem": "What is one of Branyas Morera's secrets to living a long life?", "options": ["A) Being always active on social media.", "B) Doing regular and vigorous exercise.", "C) Playing the piano from a young age.", "D) Following a specific diet every day."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题） Playing the piano from a young age.\n【定位】She also shares some of her secrets to living so long. These include playing the piano from a\nyoung age.\n她还分享了一些自己长寿的秘诀。这些秘诀包括从小弹钢琴。\n【信号】These include 引出秘诀清单。答案信号是列举句 These include playing the piano from\na young age——“These include”紧跟在“her secrets”之后，明确把弹琴列入秘诀清单，\n这种“include/such as”的列举信号是细节题的金标定位点。\n【替换】playing the piano from a young age ↔ Playing the piano from a young age\n列举项原样对应\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\n原文确实说她 active on social media，但这只是描述她的日常状态，紧接其后“These\ninclude”列举秘诀时并没有把“活跃于社交媒体”列进去；本选项盗用“active on social\nmedia”原词，却答非所问。\n借用原文：active on social media\nB) 偷换概念\n原文只说她 led an active lifestyle（生活方式活跃），并没说她做“regular and vigorous\nexercise（规律而剧烈的运动）”；本选项把“active lifestyle”偷换成程度更重的“vigorous\nexercise”，程度被拔高。\n借用原文：led an active lifestyle\nD) 与原文相反\n原文明确说她 has never followed a specific diet——从不遵循特定饮食；本选项却说她\n“每天遵循特定饮食”，与原文相反。它盗用“specific diet”原词，把否定听成肯定就会误选。\n借用原文：never followed a specific diet", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2025-12-2-l-21", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 21, "stem": "What does Branyas Morera think also contributes to a long life?", "options": ["A) Eating as little as one can possibly manage.", "B) Having the ability to forget things selectively.", "C) Figuring out the positive properties of each food item.", "D) Knowing how to remember major events in one's life."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （态度观点题） Having the ability to forget things selectively.\n【定位】She has also said that the secret to a long life is \"knowing how to choose\" what to forget.\n她还说过，长寿的秘诀是“懂得选择”要忘掉什么。\n【信号】she has also said 引出本人观点。答案信号是引述句 She has also said that the secret\nto a long life is——“she has also said”直接标记这是她本人的另一条观点，加上“also”正\n对应题干的“also contributes”，是态度观点题最可靠的定位信号。\n【替换】knowing how to choose what to forget ↔ the ability to forget things selectively\n选择遗忘换选择性遗忘\n【排除】A) 过度推断\n原文说她 always eaten little, but everything——吃得少但什么都吃；本选项却说“尽可能\n少吃”，把“eaten little”拔高到极端，还丢了“但什么都吃”的另一半。它盗用“eaten little”原\n词，制造“听到 little 就选”的诱惑。\n借用原文：always eaten little, but everything\nC) 偷换概念\n原文说酸奶对身体有 an infinite number of positive properties，是夸酸奶本身；本选项\n却说“弄清每种食物的有益属性”，把“酸奶有有益属性”偷换成“她认为要研究每种食物的属\n性”。它盗用“positive properties”原词，所指对象被偷换。\n借用原文：an infinite number of positive properties\nD) 与原文相反\n原文强调的是“懂得选择忘掉什么”，还说 Life would be impossible if we remembered\neverything——记住一切反而不行；本选项却说“懂得记住人生大事”，方向与原文相反。它\n盗用 remember/forget 同一话题词，把遗忘说成记忆。\n借用原文：Life would be impossible if we remembered everything", "points": ["听力·讲座", "态度观点题"]}, {"id": "2025-12-2-l-22", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 22, "stem": "What is of great importance to students according to the speaker?", "options": ["A) The ability to learn.", "B) The first day of school.", "C) How teachers perceive them.", "D) Where they sit in the classroom."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题） Where they sit in the classroom.\n【定位】It does matter where you sit in your classroom, so choose and choose carefully. Studies report\nthat where you sit in the classroom can impact your ability to learn and the way your teacher\nperceives you.\n你坐在教室的哪个位置，确实有影响，所以要慎重选座。研究表明，你在教室里的座位会影\n响你的学习能力以及老师对你的看法。\n【信号】加重强调 It does matter。说话人先自问 Where do you sit? Does it matter? 紧接着用加\n重语气的 It does matter where you sit in your classroom 自答，does 的强调正是答案信\n号——被反复强调的\"坐在哪\"就是说话人认定的重点。\n【替换】where you sit in your classroom ↔ Where they sit in the classroom.\n人称转换，意思不变\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\n原文确实念到 your ability to learn，但那是\"座位\"带来的\"影响之一\"，并非说话人说的\"对\n学生重要的东西\"本身。把\"被影响的结果\"当成\"重要的东西\"，是典型的听到原词就想选。\n借用原文：can impact your ability to learn\nB) 原词陷阱\n原文开头说 It's the first day of school，但那只是用来引出话题的场景背景，说话人从没\n说\"开学第一天\"对学生重要，属于借开头原词设的陷阱。\n借用原文：It's the first day of school\nC) 答非所问\n原文 the way your teacher perceives you 确被念到，但它和\"学习能力\"一样，是座位带来\n的\"影响\"，不是说话人认定\"重要\"的那样东西，张冠李戴。\n借用原文：the way your teacher perceives you", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2025-12-2-l-23", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 23, "stem": "What did one study find about students seated in the middle of the classroom?", "options": ["A) They often received close attention from the instructors.", "B) They obtained the highest grade average in their class.", "C) They actually wanted a clear vision of the blackboard.", "D) They scored higher than students sitting behind them."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题） They scored higher than students sitting behind them.\n【定位】Get this. Students sitting in the middle of the classroom also outscored students sitting\nbehind them.\n注意了：坐在教室中间的学生，成绩也超过了坐在他们后面的学生。\n【信号】提示语 Get this 引出关键发现。说话人用 Get this（\"注意听这个\"）专门提醒，随后给出\nStudents sitting in the middle of the classroom also outscored students sitting\nbehind them，这种\"提示语+发现\"正是答案信号，直接对准\"中间座位学生\"的结论。\n【替换】outscored students sitting behind them ↔ They scored higher than students sitting behind them.\noutscore = scored higher than\n【排除】A) 张冠李戴\n原文 formed a better connection with the students who sat in front 讲的是\"前排\"学生\n与老师的关系，受关注的是前排不是\"中间\"。把前排学生的待遇安到\"中间座位学生\"头上，\n是张冠李戴。\n借用原文：formed a better connection with the students who sat in front\nB) 偷换概念\n原文只说前排/中间学生 received a higher grade average（比后排更高），选项却说成\nobtained the highest grade average in their class（全班最高），把\"比较高\"偷换成\"最\n高\"，程度被夸大。\n借用原文：received a higher grade average\nC) 偷换概念\n原文 students had a clearer vision of the blackboard 说的是前排中间学生\"看黑板更清\n楚\"这一既成事实（成绩高的原因），并非选项所谓\"他们其实想要看清黑板（wanted）\"，\n既改了意思又不是本题问的发现。\n借用原文：students had a clearer vision of the blackboard", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2025-12-2-l-24", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 24, "stem": "What can eye contact, visibility and more interaction probably bring about?", "options": ["A) A higher grade average among all students of a class.", "B) A better connection between instructors and students.", "C) A stronger support for a student-centered teaching approach.", "D) A more favorable atmosphere for effective classroom learning."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题） A better connection between instructors and students.\n【定位】The instructors said that they also formed a better connection with the students who\nsat in front. Perhaps the connection is made because of eye contact and visibility and\nmore interaction.\n老师们说，他们和坐在前排的学生还建立了更好的联系。也许这种联系正是因为眼神交流、\n容易被看到以及更多的互动而形成的。\n【信号】因果倒装 because of 揭示因果。原文先给结果 formed a better connection，再用\nbecause of eye contact and visibility and more interaction 点出原因。题干把\"因\"列出\n来反问\"带来什么果\"，沿 because of 这条因果线，对应的\"果\"正是\"更好的联系\"。\n【替换】formed a better connection with the students who sat in front ↔\nA better connection between instructors and students.\n近乎原义概括，主体补全\n【排除】A) 答非所问\na higher grade average 是上一段讲座位与成绩的内容，与本题问的\"眼神交流等带来什\n么\"不是同一个因果；选项还把范围扩成 among all students of a class（全班所有人），\n既答非所问又以偏概全。\n借用原文：received a higher grade average\nC) 未提及\nA stronger support for a student-centered teaching approach. 里\"以学生为中心的教学\n法\"原文从未提及，是凭\"师生互动\"硬联想出来的，属未提及。\nD) 过度推断\nA more favorable atmosphere for effective classroom learning. 听上去与\"课堂学习\"很\n搭，但原文讲的是\"师生联系（connection）\"，从没说\"更有利的课堂学习氛围\"，属未提及\n的合理推测项。", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2025-12-2-l-25", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 25, "stem": "What do we learn about extremely shy students sitting in the front row of the classroom?", "options": ["A) They may experience some unwanted stress.", "B) They may try hard to show their preparedness.", "C) They may receive extra attention from the instructor.", "D) They may feel helpless when called on by the instructor."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题） They may experience some unwanted stress.\n【定位】So for students who are not prepared and for those who are extremely shy, the attention could\ncause some unwanted stress.\n所以，对于那些没准备好的学生以及那些极为害羞的学生来说，这种关注可能会带来一些不\n想要的压力。\n【信号】结尾 So 收束 + 转折隐含。前句还在说前排\"被老师叫到更多\"，结尾用 So for…those who\nare extremely shy, the attention could cause some unwanted stress 笔锋一转点出负面\n后果。So 引出的收束句正是针对\"害羞学生\"的答案落点。\n【替换】the attention could cause some unwanted stress ↔ They may experience some unwanted stress.\ncause→experience，因果换视角\n【排除】B) 与原文相反\nThey may try hard to show their preparedness. 原文未提；原文恰恰说的是\"没准备\n好\"的学生 students who are not prepared，与\"努力表现自己准备好了\"方向相反。\n借用原文：students who are not prepared\nC) 原词陷阱\nthe attention 确被念到，但原文说这关注对害羞学生造成的是\"压力\"；选项 They may\nreceive extra attention from the instructor. 只截取\"被额外关注\"这个中性事实，回避了问\n的\"后果\"，是听到 attention 就想选的原词陷阱。\n借用原文：you may be called on more by your teacher\nD) 过度推断\n原文说前排学生 may be called on more（更可能被点名），但只说会带来\"压力\"，并未说\n他们\"被点到时感到无助（feel helpless）\"，是顺着\"被点名\"多推了一步。\n借用原文：you may be called on more by your teacher", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2025-12-2-c-26", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 26, "stem": "", "options": ["A) actually", "B) consequently", "C) contributing", "D) fair", "E) feels", "F) manageable", "G) mends", "H) observed", "I) phase", "J) plotting", "K) quality", "L) recently", "M) taxing", "N) tends", "O) yielded"], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D) fair\n形容词\n【词性槽】it's ___ to say that …，形式主语 it 后系动词 + 表语\n【依据】It's fair to say 是英语高频习语「可以这样说、说……不为过」\nit's __(26)__ to say that music moves people in special ways. 前文用「有些音乐让你想\n跳舞，有些激励你改变世界」铺陈，再来一句「可以说音乐以特别的方式打动人心」做总\n结，正是 it's fair to say 的标准用法。\n【竞争词】F) manageable manageable 修饰「可控、可处理」，和 to say 接不上\nmanageable 通常修饰任务、症状（make symptoms more manageable），用来说一件事\n「可控」；说一句话「可控」语义荒谬。\nM) taxing taxing 是「费神的」，说一句话费神在语境里不通\ntaxing 多修饰任务、工作量；it's taxing to say 字面虽通，但与前文铺陈「音乐打动人」的语气\n完全相反，开篇就泼冷水不合逻辑。", "points": ["选词填空", "形容词"]}, {"id": "2025-12-2-c-27", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 27, "stem": "", "options": ["A) actually", "B) consequently", "C) contributing", "D) fair", "E) feels", "F) manageable", "G) mends", "H) observed", "I) phase", "J) plotting", "K) quality", "L) recently", "M) taxing", "N) tends", "O) yielded"], "answer": "E", "analysis": "【答案】E) feels\n动词\n【词性槽】your brain does … which ___ to you like a sting，which 引导的定语从句缺谓语\n【依据】feel like 是固定搭配「感觉像」\nwhich __(27)__ to you like a sting in your brain 后面跟 like a sting（像针刺），feels\n(to you) like … 正是「（对你来说）感觉像……」的标准表达，对人称 sb. 用 feels to sb.\nlike 也通。\n【竞争词】G) mends mend 是「修补」，与 like a sting 不搭\nmend 通常修补衣服、关系，无法「mend to you like a sting」，搭配和语义都不成立。\nN) tends tend 后接 to do，不接 to you like\ntends 常见结构是 tends to do sth.（往往做某事），后面接动词原形；这里后面是介词 to you\n+ like + 名词，结构对不上。", "points": ["选词填空", "动词"]}, {"id": "2025-12-2-c-28", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 28, "stem": "", "options": ["A) actually", "B) consequently", "C) contributing", "D) fair", "E) feels", "F) manageable", "G) mends", "H) observed", "I) phase", "J) plotting", "K) quality", "L) recently", "M) taxing", "N) tends", "O) yielded"], "answer": "L", "analysis": "【答案】L) recently\n副词\n【词性槽】a ___ published study，冠词与过去分词之间填副词修饰 published\n【依据】recently published「最近发表」是学术文献标配搭配\nAccording to a __(28)__ published study，引用研究时强调「最近发表的一项研究」是英\n语中极高频的表达 a recently published study；时间副词 recently 与 published 在意义\n上紧密呼应。\n【竞争词】A) actually actually 表「实际上」，修饰 published 语义空洞\nactually published「实际上发表的」在英语里几乎不用——研究要么发表了要么没有，没有\n「实际上发表」这种说法，搭配不自然。\nB) consequently consequently 表因果，前文无因，逻辑不接\nconsequently 用来连接前因后果，这里 According to a … published study 是引出依据，并非\n承接因果；且 a consequently published study 不是英语习惯搭配。\n【易错】最具迷惑性的错误词是 A) —— L 还是 A？三个副词都「看着能用」。a __(28)__\npublished study 中考生可能凭直觉填 actually 表「实际上发表」，但英语习惯是 recently\npublished；防坑点：a + 时间副词 + V-ed 名词是学术语境的强搭配模板，时间副词优先\n于强调副词。", "points": ["选词填空", "副词"]}, {"id": "2025-12-2-c-29", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 29, "stem": "", "options": ["A) actually", "B) consequently", "C) contributing", "D) fair", "E) feels", "F) manageable", "G) mends", "H) observed", "I) phase", "J) plotting", "K) quality", "L) recently", "M) taxing", "N) tends", "O) yielded"], "answer": "N", "analysis": "【答案】N) tends\n动词\n【词性槽】Alzheimer's ___ to put people …，主语后缺谓语，第三人称单数\n【依据】tends to do 表「倾向于、往往」，契合阿尔茨海默症常见症状\nAlzheimer's __(29)__ to put people into layers of confusion，「阿尔茨海默症往往让人\n陷入层层困惑」，tends to 表常发性、规律性，符合医学论述的口吻。\n【竞争词】G) mends mend 不接 to do，且语义反向\nmend 是「修补、痊愈」，不接 to do；而且 Alzheimer's mends people 意思是「阿尔茨海默\n症治愈人」，与下文「陷入困惑」完全矛盾。", "points": ["选词填空", "动词"]}, {"id": "2025-12-2-c-30", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 30, "stem": "", "options": ["A) actually", "B) consequently", "C) contributing", "D) fair", "E) feels", "F) manageable", "G) mends", "H) observed", "I) phase", "J) plotting", "K) quality", "L) recently", "M) taxing", "N) tends", "O) yielded"], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A) actually\n副词\n【词性槽】music can sometimes ___ lift people out of …，情态动词 + 副词 + 实义动词\n【依据】actually 强调「真的、的确」，呼应前文研究证实\nthe study confirms that music can sometimes __(30)__ lift people out of the\nAlzheimer's haze，研究「证实」音乐有时确实能把患者从混沌中拉出来，actually 强调这\n种效果是真实存在的，与 confirms 的语气一致。\n【竞争词】B) consequently consequently 是因果连接副词，本句无因果链\nconsequently 通常放句首/分句首引出结果（…, consequently, …）；这里没有「因为某事 →\n所以 lift」的因果，硬塞进 can sometimes consequently lift 既不合习惯也不合逻辑。", "points": ["选词填空", "副词"]}, {"id": "2025-12-2-c-31", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 31, "stem": "", "options": ["A) actually", "B) consequently", "C) contributing", "D) fair", "E) feels", "F) manageable", "G) mends", "H) observed", "I) phase", "J) plotting", "K) quality", "L) recently", "M) taxing", "N) tends", "O) yielded"], "answer": "H", "analysis": "【答案】H) observed\n动词\n【词性槽】This phenomenon has been ___ several times，被动语态，has been + 过去分词\n【依据】observed「被观察到」与并列的 studied 同属研究动作\nThis phenomenon has been __(31)__ several times but rarely studied properly. 中 but\n连接转折，前后都讲科研动作：「被观察到多次，却很少被认真研究」，observed 与\nstudied 是天然的近义并列。\n【竞争词】O) yielded yield 不及物或表「产生/屈服」，与 phenomenon 主语不搭\nyield 表「产生（结果）」时用法是 A yielded B；表「屈服」时是 yield to。This\nphenomenon has been yielded 字面意思「这种现象被产生了」语义荒谬，主被动语义都对\n不上。", "points": ["选词填空", "动词"]}, {"id": "2025-12-2-c-32", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 32, "stem": "", "options": ["A) actually", "B) consequently", "C) contributing", "D) fair", "E) feels", "F) manageable", "G) mends", "H) observed", "I) phase", "J) plotting", "K) quality", "L) recently", "M) taxing", "N) tends", "O) yielded"], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C) contributing\n形容词\n【词性槽】___ author on the study，冠词位 + 名词 author 前作定语\n【依据】contributing author 是学术文献固定身份「参与撰写的作者」\nassociate professor in Radiology at the University of Utah and __(32)__ author on the\nstudy，介绍 Jeff Anderson 的双重身份，「副教授」+「该研究的参与撰写作者」逻辑自\n然；contributing author 是学术论文署名中的标准说法。\n【竞争词】J) plotting plotting 是「密谋/绘图」，修饰 author 语义荒诞\nplotting author 字面是「正在密谋的作者」或「在画图的作者」，作为学术身份描述完全不\n通；学术场合无此搭配。\nM) taxing taxing 形容「繁重的」，不能用来形容人的身份\ntaxing 通常修饰任务、工作（a taxing job），用来形容 author（人）逻辑不通，且语义偏负\n面，与正面介绍学者的语境冲突。", "points": ["选词填空", "形容词"]}, {"id": "2025-12-2-c-33", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 33, "stem": "", "options": ["A) actually", "B) consequently", "C) contributing", "D) fair", "E) feels", "F) manageable", "G) mends", "H) observed", "I) phase", "J) plotting", "K) quality", "L) recently", "M) taxing", "N) tends", "O) yielded"], "answer": "M", "analysis": "【答案】M) taxing\n动词\n【词性槽】the diagnoses of dementia are ___ resources，be 动词后缺谓语成分，与后面\nresources 形成动宾\n【依据】tax resources「使资源吃紧」是固定搭配，与 to the maximum 呼应\nthe diagnoses of dementia are __(33)__ resources to the maximum，「痴呆诊断正在\n最大限度地消耗资源」；tax 作动词意为「使……负担沉重」，tax resources 在医疗/财政语\n境是标准说法，to the maximum「到极限」直接强化这种「榨干资源」的意味。\n【竞争词】J) plotting plot resources 不是搭配，且语义对不上「占用资源」\nplot 表「策划/绘制」，plot resources 字面像「策划资源、绘制资源」，与「消耗社会资源」\n的语境完全错位。\n【易错】最具迷惑性的错误词是 J) —— M 还是 J？「熟词僻义」盲区。are __(33)__ resources to\nthe maximum 中考生若不知 tax 还有动词义「使吃紧」，会怀疑 taxing，转去试\nplotting；防坑点：看到 resources、energy、patience 等被「耗」对象，taxing 比\nplotting 自然得多，平时多积累一词多义。", "points": ["选词填空", "动词"]}, {"id": "2025-12-2-c-34", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 34, "stem": "", "options": ["A) actually", "B) consequently", "C) contributing", "D) fair", "E) feels", "F) manageable", "G) mends", "H) observed", "I) phase", "J) plotting", "K) quality", "L) recently", "M) taxing", "N) tends", "O) yielded"], "answer": "F", "analysis": "【答案】F) manageable\n形容词\n【词性槽】make the symptoms more ___，make + 宾语 + 形容词比较级，空填形容词\n【依据】make symptoms more manageable「让症状更可控」医学语境标配\nit might make the symptoms more __(34)__, decrease the cost of care and improve a\npatient's __(35)__ of life.，三件好事并列：症状更可控、护理成本下降、生活质量提升；\nmanageable 与「降低成本、改善质量」语气完全一致。", "points": ["选词填空", "形容词"]}, {"id": "2025-12-2-c-35", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 35, "stem": "", "options": ["A) actually", "B) consequently", "C) contributing", "D) fair", "E) feels", "F) manageable", "G) mends", "H) observed", "I) phase", "J) plotting", "K) quality", "L) recently", "M) taxing", "N) tends", "O) yielded"], "answer": "K", "analysis": "【答案】K) quality\n名词\n【词性槽】improve a patient's ___ of life，所有格+of 锁定名词\n【依据】quality of life 是英语固定搭配\nimprove a patient's __(35)__ of life 中，quality of life「生活质量」是医学/社科语境里的\n高频固定表达，与前文 decrease the cost of care 并列，逻辑顺。\n【竞争词】I) phase phase of life 偏「人生阶段」，与「改善」不搭\nphase of life 表示「人生的某个阶段」，可以「进入」一个 phase 但谈不上 improve a phase\nof life；这里要的是生活的「品质」可以被提升，phase 在搭配上和语义上都对不上。", "points": ["选词填空", "名词"]}, {"id": "2025-12-2-m-36", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 36, "stem": "Certain gaming skills meet the requirements of the armed forces in Britain.", "options": null, "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】英国武装部队\n【定位】Even the military is hiring gamers. \"The ability to absorb information, react swiftly and\ncoordinate actions whilst remaining calm under pressure are often attributes of people that are\ngood at gaming,\" according to a Royal Air Force spokesperson.\n连军方都在招玩家。皇家空军发言人说：吸收信息、快速反应、在压力下保持冷静同时协调\n行动的能力，往往是擅长打游戏的人身上具备的特质。\n【改写】the armed forces in Britain ↔ Royal Air Force\n上位 vs 下位的具体代表\nCertain gaming skills meet the requirements ↔\nSkills acquired through gaming can be very relevant to certain areas\nmeet the requirements 与 be relevant to certain areas 同义", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2025-12-2-m-37", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 37, "stem": "One veteran player has applied his gaming skills to his real-world business and owes his success partly to a game company.", "options": null, "answer": "I", "analysis": "【答案】I 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】老玩家 + 把成功部分归功于游戏公司\n【定位】He credits his success to his family, his strong desire to run his own business — and \"a damn\ngood company in Iceland that made a damn good game\".\n他把自己的成功归功于家人、强烈的自主创业愿望，以及一家在冰岛做出好游戏的好公司。\n【改写】has applied his gaming skills to his real-world business ↔\nhe realised he could transfer his skills to real-life business\napply ... to 与 transfer ... to 同义\nowes his success partly to a game company ↔\nHe credits his success to his family, his strong desire to run his own business — and \"a damn\ngood company in Iceland that made a damn good game\"\ncredit success to 与 owe success to 同义；partly 体现在并列归因\n【辨邻】 G Why can't we put in our résumé our gaming victories and achievements? 只是建\n议玩家用游戏成就求职，并没有出现 Ricci 那种把虚拟公司经验落到 Zentech 的实际\n创业故事，更没有把功劳归给游戏公司，与题干两个并列条件都对不上。\nWhy can't we put in our résumé our gaming victories and achievements?", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2025-12-2-m-38", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 38, "stem": "According to a researcher, bad reputation about games will probably limit a gamer's chances of landing a job.", "options": null, "answer": "F", "analysis": "【答案】F 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】研究者 + 坏名声会限制求职\n【定位】For now, I think the negative reputation around games means that telling someone you're a\ndetermined gamer is more likely to harm your chances of getting a job.\n目前我觉得游戏的负面名声意味着，跟别人说自己是个铁杆玩家更可能损害你找工作的\n机会。\n【改写】bad reputation about games ↔ the negative reputation around games\nbad 与 negative 同义；about 与 around 此处用法相同\nlimit a gamer's chances of landing a job ↔ harm your chances of getting a job\nlimit 与 harm 同义；landing a job 与 getting a job 同义", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2025-12-2-m-39", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 39, "stem": "Businesses are beginning to realise gaming skills are applicable to their employees' work.", "options": null, "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】企业开始意识到游戏技能可用\n【定位】Businesses are waking up to the skills gamers can bring to the workplace.\n企业开始意识到玩家能带到职场的那些技能。\n【改写】Businesses are beginning to realise gaming skills are applicable to their employees' work ↔\nBusinesses are waking up to the skills gamers can bring to the workplace\nwake up to 与 begin to realise 同义；applicable to ... work 与 bring to the workplace 同义\n【辨邻】 C there is already a growing acceptance that gaming skills are transferable 谈的是\n社会层面接受度的提升，主语不是 Businesses，也没有 wake up to / begin to\nrealise 那种觉醒意味。题干强调的是企业主体的认知变化，与本句视角不符。\nthere is already a growing acceptance that gaming skills are transferable", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2025-12-2-m-40", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 40, "stem": "A physicist admits gaming has contributed to his computer knowledge and skills of solving problems.", "options": null, "answer": "K", "analysis": "【答案】K 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】物理师 + 电脑知识 + 解决问题\n【定位】Instead, he credits old-school games like Palace of Magic with exposing him to computers.\nEvery new gaming upgrade improved his knowledge of how they worked. ... \"Most\nvideogames are essentially puzzles to solve,\" he says. \"And problem-solving is a big part of\nmy job.\"\n相反，他把自己对计算机的接触归功于 Palace of Magic 这类老牌游戏，每一次硬件升级都\n加深了他对电脑工作方式的认识。他还说大多数电子游戏本质上都是要解的谜，而解决问题\n正是他工作中的大头。\n【改写】A physicist ↔ a radiotherapy physicist with the NHS in Surrey\n上位 vs 下位\ngaming has contributed to his computer knowledge ↔\nEvery new gaming upgrade improved his knowledge of how they worked\ncontribute to 与 improve 同义\nskills of solving problems ↔ problem-solving is a big part of my job\nsolving problems 与 problem-solving 同形不同写", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2025-12-2-m-41", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 41, "stem": "Game Academy proposes to help gamers develop their skills using critical thinking.", "options": null, "answer": "G", "analysis": "【答案】G 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】Game Academy + 批判性思维\n【定位】But part of Game Academy's suggestion is to transform casual gamers into \"conscious\" ones,\napplying critical thinking to developing their skills.\n但 Game Academy 的建议中有一部分是把休闲玩家变成有意识的玩家，把批判性思维用到\n技能培养上去。\n【改写】Game Academy proposes to help gamers develop their skills using critical thinking ↔\npart of Game Academy's suggestion is to transform casual gamers into \"conscious\" ones,\napplying critical thinking to developing their skills\npropose 与 suggestion 同族；develop their skills 与 developing their skills 同语；using\ncritical thinking 与 applying critical thinking 同义\n【辨邻】 C analyse gamers' habits from their online gaming profile, and offer courses in\nvaluable skills that reflect their aptitudes 是 Game Academy 的另一项做法陈述，\n专名相同，但不是题干要的批判性思维方向。\nanalyse gamers' habits from their online gaming profile, and offer courses in valuable skills that\nreflect their aptitudes", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2025-12-2-m-42", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 42, "stem": "One who enjoys playing games that require management skills to win might possess more qualities of a manager.", "options": null, "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】玩管理类游戏的人具有管理者特质\n【定位】But if you prefer Civilization, Total War, or X-Com, where strategy and resource management\nare key, then you might have more in common with managers.\n但如果你更喜欢《文明》《全面战争》《幽浮》这类以策略和资源管理为核心的游戏，那你\n和管理者的共同点可能就更多了。\n【改写】games that require management skills to win ↔\nCivilization, Total War, or X-Com, where strategy and resource management are key\n用具体游戏 + 关键属性描述等价于抽象技能名\nmight possess more qualities of a manager ↔ you might have more in common with managers\npossess qualities of a manager 与 have more in common with managers 同义\n【辨邻】 E There are plenty of soft skills that gamers can utilise in a professional setting,\nsuch as teamwork, problem solving and strategic planning 给出的是一组与具体\n岗位无关的软技能清单，没有像题干那样把某类游戏与管理者气质直接挂钩。\nThere are plenty of soft skills that gamers can utilise in a professional setting, such as teamwork,\nproblem solving and strategic planning", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2025-12-2-m-43", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 43, "stem": "In more conventional views, gaming is a time-wasting activity pursued by young boys.", "options": null, "answer": "J", "analysis": "【答案】J 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】传统观念 + 浪费时间 + 男生爱好\n【定位】In more traditional settings, gaming is still imagined to be the pursuit of teenage boys — a\nwaste of time.\n在更传统的认知里，玩游戏仍然被想成是青少年男生的爱好——是浪费时间。\n【改写】In more conventional views ↔ In more traditional settings\nconventional 与 traditional 同义\ngaming is a time-wasting activity ↔ a waste of time\n名词短语 a waste of time 与形容词 time-wasting 同义\npursued by young boys ↔ the pursuit of teenage boys\n动词被动 vs 名词形态；teenage 与 young 同义", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2025-12-2-m-44", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 44, "stem": "It is suggested that gaming skills be included in a résumé if they enhance a job applicant's appeal.", "options": null, "answer": "E", "analysis": "【答案】E 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】简历该不该写游戏 + 提升候选人吸引力\n【定位】\"It's about how you either make it relevant to the job you are applying for, or how it makes\nyou more interesting as a potential employee,\" Mr Gardner says.\nGardner 先生说：关键就在于你要么让它（游戏战绩）和你申请的岗位挂上钩，要么让它使\n你作为候选人更有吸引力。\n【改写】gaming skills be included in a résumé ↔\ndoes that really mean your top 100 ranking in Overwatch should go down in your résumé?\ninclude in a résumé 与 go down in your résumé 同义\nenhance a job applicant's appeal ↔ how it makes you more interesting as a potential employee\nenhance applicant's appeal 与 make you more interesting as a potential employee 同义\n【辨邻】 G Why can't we put in our résumé our gaming victories and achievements? 是无条\n件鼓励写进简历，没有 if they enhance a job applicant's appeal 这种限定；题干强\n调的恰恰是这个限定，所以邻段对不上题干。\nWhy can't we put in our résumé our gaming victories and achievements?", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2025-12-2-m-45", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 45, "stem": "Many gamers know their passion for playing games has shaped their identity.", "options": null, "answer": "H", "analysis": "【答案】H 段（弱锚点）\n【锚点】玩家自知游戏塑造了自己\n【定位】There are already plenty of gamers out there who know that their hobby has made them who\nthey are.\n已经有不少玩家清楚自己这份爱好塑造了今天的自己。\n【改写】Many gamers know ↔ plenty of gamers out there who know\nMany 与 plenty of 同义；都接 gamers ... know\ntheir passion for playing games has shaped their identity ↔\ntheir hobby has made them who they are\npassion for playing games 与 hobby 同指；shaped their identity 与 made them who they\nare 同义\n【辨邻】 I Mr Ricci, who had always dreamed of being the boss of his own company, ran\nan in-game corporation comprising hundreds of players. Eventually, he realised\nhe could transfer his skills to real-life business 是一个玩家把技能落到生意的个例\n叙述，不是 Many gamers 群体自知。\nMr Ricci, who had always dreamed of being the boss of his own company, ran an in-game\ncorporation comprising hundreds of players. Eventually, he realised he could transfer his skills to\nreal-life business\nK he credits old-school games like Palace of Magic with exposing him to\ncomputers. Every new gaming upgrade improved his knowledge of how they\nworked 谈的是一个人具体的计算机知识来源，主语 he 是单数，立意也偏具体技能而\n非身份塑造。\nhe credits old-school games like Palace of Magic with exposing him to computers. Every new\ngaming upgrade improved his knowledge of how they worked", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2025-12-2-r-46", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 46, "stem": "What do we learn about advertising campaigns from the snack food companies?", "options": ["A) They convey the idea that their products are ok if consumers exercise more.", "B) They send the message that bad diets are responsible for Americans' obesity.", "C) They usually feature sports stars to emphasize the benefits of their snack foods.", "D) They claim that lack of exercise is to blame for the increasing obesity rates."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题）\n【判型】What do we learn about advertising campaigns from the snack food\ncompanies? 是 What do we learn about X 句式，问的是关于 X 的已知事实，不\n含 infer/suggest/imply。\n【拆句】What do we learn about advertising campaigns from the snack food companies? 主\n干 What do we learn about X，X = advertising campaigns，后面 from the snack\nfood companies 是介词短语修饰 campaigns，结构平铺无陷阱。\n【定位】Advertising campaigns from the snack food companies, often featuring sports stars, send the\nmessage that we can neutralize any negative effects of consuming their products simply by\ngetting more physical exercise.\n零食公司的广告活动经常以体育明星为主角，传递的信息是：只要多做体育锻炼，就能抵消\n食用其产品带来的任何负面影响。\n【选项】A) ✓ 正确\nwe can neutralize any negative effects of consuming their products simply by getting more\nphysical exercise\n↔ their products are ok if consumers exercise more\n预判\"广告称多运动就能抵消坏处\"和 A 的 their products are ok if consumers\nexercise more 完全对得上。\nB) 张冠李戴\nThey send the message that bad diets are responsible for Americans' obesity. 说\n的是广告传达\"坏饮食才是肥胖元凶\"——但这其实是 Bad diets are 这句作者引述研究\n的结论，与广告立场恰恰相反。\nC) 偷换概念\nThey usually feature sports stars to emphasize the benefits of their snack foods.\n说请明星是为了\"强调零食本身的好处\"——但原文 often featuring sports stars 后接的\n逻辑是\"运动能抵消坏处\"，明星是\"运动\"的化身，不是为吹嘘零食优点。\nD) 与原文相反\nThey claim that lack of exercise is to blame for the increasing obesity rates. 说广\n告主张\"缺乏锻炼导致肥胖\"——这是后文研究的反方观点 a lack of exercise is not to\nblame 的镜像，而且原文是 not to blame，D 偷偷把 not 去掉。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2025-12-2-r-47", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 47, "stem": "What does the passage say about health education?", "options": ["A) It has shed light on teens' instinct for rebellion and autonomy.", "B) It has placed much more emphasis on cure than on prevention.", "C) It has contributed little to the campaigns against obesity.", "D) It has largely failed to turn teens away from junk food."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题）\n【判型】What does the passage say about health education? 直接问\"文章关于 X 怎么说\"，要\n求复述事实。\n【拆句】What does the passage say about health education? 主干 What does the passage\nsay about X，X = health education，结构无陷阱。\n【定位】Unfortunately, while health education has shown some success among young children, teens\nhave been harder to reach.\n遗憾的是，虽然健康教育在低龄儿童中取得了一定成效，但对青少年的影响一直很难触达。\n【选项】A) 张冠李戴\nIt has shed light on teens' instinct for rebellion and autonomy. 说健康教育\"揭示了\n青少年的叛逆本性\"——但 instinct for rebelliousness and autonomy 是第三段的\nnew intervention 才用到的概念，与第二段 health education 无关。\nB) 偷换概念\nIt has placed much more emphasis on cure than on prevention. 说健康教育\"更重\n治疗轻预防\"——但原文 Prevention is better than cure because obesity is so\ndifficult to treat. 是作者对干预总体的观点，并未说健康教育\"更重治疗\"。\nC) 以偏概全\nIt has contributed little to the campaigns against obesity. 全盘否定健康教育，但原\n文 health education has shown some success among young children 明确承认对\n儿童有成效，并非\"几乎无贡献\"。\nD) ✓ 正确\nteens have been harder to reach ↔ largely failed to turn teens away from junk food\nUnfortunately ↔ largely failed\n预判\"对青少年没奏效\"与 D 的 largely failed to turn teens away from junk food 完全\n契合。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2025-12-2-r-48", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 48, "stem": "What is a major flaw in previous interventions to help reduce junk food consumption?", "options": ["A) They motivated teens to stay away from junk food only temporarily.", "B) They focused on the benefits young people would reap in the future.", "C) They were based on the assumption that motivation was the only key.", "D) They were incapable of appealing to teens' instinct for rebelliousness."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题）\n【判型】What is a major flaw in previous interventions to help reduce junk food\nconsumption? 直问 What is，要的是文中明确写出的具体缺陷。\n【拆句】What is a major flaw in previous interventions to help reduce junk food\nconsumption? 主干 What is a major flaw，后跟两层修饰：in previous interventions 限\n定缺陷的归属，to help reduce junk food consumption 是不定式修饰 interventions 的\n功能。要看清 flaw 修饰的是 previous interventions，不是别的。\n【定位】The researchers argued that previous interventions have probably been unsuccessful because\nof a major flaw: they focused on a future, healthier you and assumed that this would be\nenough motivation for teenagers.\n研究者认为，以往的干预之所以可能失败，是因为一个重大缺陷：它们把重心放在\"未来更\n健康的你\"，并假设这就足以激励青少年。\n【选项】A) 无中生有\nThey motivated teens to stay away from junk food only temporarily. 说\"只在短\n期有效\"——但原文从未提及干预的\"短期/长期\"时效问题，only temporarily 属凭空\n捏造。\nB) ✓ 正确\nfocused on a future, healthier you ↔\nfocused on the benefits young people would reap in the future\n预判\"押注未来好处\"和 B 的 focused on the benefits young people would reap in\nthe future 完全契合。\nC) 过度推断\nThey were based on the assumption that motivation was the only key. 把原文\nassumed that this would be enough motivation 扭成\"动机是唯一关键\"——原文只\n说\"这一点已经足够\"，未说\"动机是唯一\"。\nD) 张冠李戴\nThey were incapable of appealing to teens' instinct for rebelliousness. 说\"以前的\n干预无法触动叛逆本能\"——叛逆本性是 new intervention cleverly exploits\nteenagers' instinct for rebelliousness 的卖点，反推回去\"以前没用\"是考生自己脑补\n的，原文并未把\"未利用叛逆本能\"列为 flaw。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2025-12-2-r-49", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 49, "stem": "In what way is the new intervention innovative?", "options": ["A) It emphasizes people's sense of social responsibility.", "B) It cleverly exploits teenagers' personal weaknesses.", "C) It takes advantage of teenagers' natural inclination.", "D) It promises immediate rather than future benefits."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题）\n【判型】In what way is the new intervention innovative? 问的是\"以何种方式\"，答案在原文有具\n体描述。\n【拆句】In what way is the new intervention innovative? 主干 the new intervention is\ninnovative，前置 In what way 提问。innovative 是表语形容词，整句结构简单。\n【定位】In contrast, the new intervention cleverly exploits teenagers' instinct for rebelliousness and\nautonomy, and the value they place on social justice.\n与之相对，新干预巧妙地利用了青少年对叛逆和自主的本能，以及他们对社会公正的重视。\n【选项】A) 偷换概念\nIt emphasizes people's sense of social responsibility. 只抓住 the value they place\non social justice 这一点，且把 teenagers 扩大成 people、把 social justice 偷换成\nsocial responsibility。\nB) 偷换概念\nIt cleverly exploits teenagers' personal weaknesses. 把 instinct for rebelliousness\nand autonomy 这一中性偏正面的\"本能\"扭曲成 personal weaknesses（个人弱点）；\n原文并未把叛逆视为\"弱点\"。\nC) ✓ 正确\nexploits teenagers' instinct for rebelliousness and autonomy ↔\ntakes advantage of teenagers' natural inclination\n预判\"顺势利用青少年天性\"和 C 的 takes advantage of teenagers' natural inclination\n一一对应。\nD) 过度推断\nIt promises immediate rather than future benefits. 说新干预\"承诺即时好处\"——但\n原文从未提\"即时好处\"，且作者把\"许诺未来好处\"作为 previous interventions 的缺\n陷，并不意味着新干预走\"即时好处\"的反向路线。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2025-12-2-r-50", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 50, "stem": "What conclusion can be drawn from the new research?", "options": ["A) A totally different approach could be adopted to combat advertising campaigns for snack food.", "B) An effective intervention has to be found to persuade teenagers to switch to a healthier lifestyle.", "C) Obesity can be hopefully prevented by building links between teenagers' values and healthy eating.", "D) An innovative way to beat obesity is to expose food companies' manipulative and deceptive practices."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （主旨题）\n【判型】What conclusion can be drawn from the new research? 让你为整篇研究下结论，覆盖\n全文而非某一段。\n【拆句】What conclusion can be drawn from the new research? 被动结构 conclusion can be\ndrawn from X，相当于 What conclusion does the new research lead to。注意 drawn\nfrom 是\"从…得出\"，不要看反。\n【选项】A) 以偏概全\nA totally different approach could be adopted to combat advertising campaigns\nfor snack food. 把\"反击广告\"当作研究结论——但 fight back against the companies\nby buying and eating less processed food 只是干预里使用的\"虚构问卷\"内容，是实\n验素材，不是研究结论。\nB) 与原文相反\nAn effective intervention has to be found to persuade teenagers to switch to a\nhealthier lifestyle. 把\"得找到有效干预\"作为结论——但本研究本身已经提出并验证了一\n种有效干预，结论是\"这条路有希望\"而不是\"还得继续找\"。\nC) ✓ 正确\nassociating a healthy diet with teenagers' own values seems to be a promising avenue to prevent\nobesity\n↔\nObesity can be hopefully prevented by building links between teenagers' values and\nhealthy eating.\nteenagers' own values ↔ teenagers' values\nC 既包含\"青少年价值观\"又包含\"健康饮食\"和\"防肥胖\"三个要素，正好对应主线串出的\n结论。\nD) 以偏概全\nAn innovative way to beat obesity is to expose food companies' manipulative\nand deceptive practices. 把\"揭露食品公司套路\"当结论——但 the manipulative and\ndeceptive strategies 只是新干预里给学生看的材料之一，并非研究结论本身。研究结\n论是\"把饮食与青少年价值观挂钩\"，揭露公司只是其中的具体手段。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "主旨题"]}, {"id": "2025-12-2-r-51", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 51, "stem": "What do scientists think of REM?", "options": ["A) It is a sign of dreaming in adults and infants alike.", "B) It is essential to human beings' language learning.", "C) It determines the formation of connections in the brain.", "D) It performs a different function in babies than in adults."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题）\n【判型】What do scientists think of REM? 直接问科学家对 REM 的看法，是对原文具体观点的客\n观转述。\n【拆句】Q51 结构是 \"What do scientists think of REM?\"，主谓宾极简，think of 在此意为\n\"对……的看法\"，无陷阱。\n【定位】But scientists believe REM serves a completely different purpose for newborns and infants\nthan dreaming.\n但科学家认为，对新生儿和婴儿而言，REM 所起的作用与做梦完全不同。\n【选项】A) 与原文相反\n原文明说婴儿在 REM 期\"不做梦\"，It is a sign of dreaming in adults and infants\nalike 反而说成人婴儿都做梦，与文意相反。\nB) 以偏概全\n原文 eventually, learn languages 只把语言学习列为大脑发育的一个结果，It is\nessential to human beings' language learning 把它拔高为 REM 的本质功能，范围\n被放大。\nC) 偷换概念\n原文是 REM \"allows\" 婴儿大脑发育连接（提供条件），It determines the formation\nof connections in the brain 把 \"提供条件\" 偷换为 \"决定\"，强度被改。\nD) ✓ 正确\na completely different purpose for newborns and infants than dreaming ↔\na different function in babies than in adults\n预判 \"REM 在婴儿与成人身上作用不同\" 对应 It performs a different function in\nbabies than in adults.，两端同义替换链完整。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2025-12-2-r-52", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 52, "stem": "What can we infer about nightmares from the passage?", "options": ["A) They occur often in people with an active imagination.", "B) They occur in people suffering from emotional stress.", "C) They vary in different stages of brain development.", "D) They appear in the sleep of both adults and infants."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （推断题）\n【判型】What can we infer about nightmares from the passage? 含 infer，属推断而非细节。\n【拆句】Q52 主干 \"What can we infer about nightmares\"，about 引出推断对象，from the\npassage 是状语，结构简单。\n【定位】Nightmares stem from exposure to trauma(创伤), an overactive imagination, and the normal\nstresses of everyday life.\n噩梦源于创伤经历、过度活跃的想象，以及日常生活中的正常压力。\n【选项】A) 以偏概全\nP3 只把 an overactive imagination 列为三因之一，They occur often in people with\nan active imagination 把它单独抽出且加 often 强化，把一项当作主因。\nB) ✓ 正确\nthe normal stresses of everyday life ↔ emotional stress\n预判 \"承受情绪压力的人易做噩梦\" 与 They occur in people suffering from\nemotional stress. 同义。\nC) 无中生有\n原文 P3 谈噩梦的成因，从未把噩梦与 \"大脑发育阶段\" 的关系作为话题；They vary in\ndifferent stages of brain development 把别处的 brain development 概念硬套到\nnightmares 上。\nD) 与原文相反\n原文 it's safe to assume babies do not have nightmares, either 明确婴儿不做噩\n梦，They appear in the sleep of both adults and infants 反着说 \"成人婴儿睡眠中都\n出现\"。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "推断题"]}, {"id": "2025-12-2-r-53", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 53, "stem": "What is the aim of psychologist David Foulkes' study?", "options": ["A) To reveal the secrets of children's dreams.", "B) To see if children start dreaming at age two.", "C) To solve the mystery of dreaming and brain growth.", "D) To confirm the general consensus on when kids dream."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题）\n【判型】What is the aim of psychologist David Foulkes' study? 直接问研究目的，是对原文目的\n句的客观转述。\n【拆句】Q53 结构是 \"What is the aim of psychologist David Foulkes' study?\"，of 短语限定\naim，psychologist 是 David Foulkes 的同位身份说明，无陷阱。\n【定位】Psychologist David Foulkes studies children (from very small kids to teens) to bring the\nsecrets of their dreams to the light of day.\n心理学家 David Foulkes 研究儿童（从年幼孩童到青少年），目的是把他们梦境的秘密揭示\n于人前。\n【选项】A) ✓ 正确\nto bring the secrets of their dreams to the light of day ↔\nTo reveal the secrets of children's dreams.\n预判 \"揭示儿童梦境秘密\" 与 To reveal the secrets of children's dreams. 一一对应，\n选 A。\nB) 偷换概念\n原文 they start dreaming around the age of two 是研究背景里的 \"普遍共识\"，并非\nFoulkes 研究的目的；To see if children start dreaming at age two 把背景信息当成\n研究目的。\nC) 无中生有\n原文未把 Foulkes 的目标说成 \"解开做梦与大脑发育之谜\"；To solve the mystery of\ndreaming and brain growth 引入了 P2 的 brain 概念硬接到 Foulkes 研究目的上。\nD) 偷换概念\n原文 The general consensus is that they start dreaming around the age of two. 表\n示这是研究界已有共识，Foulkes 并非为证实它而研究；To confirm the general\nconsensus on when kids dream 把 \"已存在的共识\" 当成 Foulkes 要证实的对象。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2025-12-2-r-54", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 54, "stem": "What are Foulkes' findings about kids' dreams?", "options": ["A) They feature strange animals.", "B) They are unsurprising to adults.", "C) They grow and evolve with age.", "D) They reflect kids' waking life."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题）\n【判型】What are Foulkes' findings about kids' dreams? 直接问研究结论是什么，是细节题。\n【拆句】Q54 结构是 \"What are Foulkes' findings about kids' dreams?\"，about kids' dreams\n限定 findings 的范围，没有从句也没有否定，无陷阱。\n【定位】Foulkes' findings are unsurprising. Basically, little kids have little dreams. But exactly what\nkids see while dreaming depends on their age. As children develop and grow, their dreams\ndo too.\nFoulkes 的发现并不令人意外。本质上，小孩做的也是小梦。但孩子在梦中究竟看到什么，\n取决于他们的年龄。随着孩子的发育成长，他们的梦也在发育成长。\n【选项】A) 以偏概全\nThey heavily feature animals and other familiar sights 只说幼儿梦多含动物等\"熟悉\n景象\"，They feature strange animals 把动物拔成 \"strange animals\" 且当成所有孩子\n梦的特征。\nB) 答非所问\n题问 findings 的内容；They are unsurprising to adults. 拿原文 \"unsurprising\" 的评\n价词当做内容，Foulkes' findings are unsurprising 的 unsurprising 是对 \"findings 本\n身\" 的评价，而非 findings 的实质内容。\nC) ✓ 正确\nAs children develop and grow, their dreams do too ↔ They grow and evolve with age.\n预判 \"梦随孩子发育而发育\" 与 They grow and evolve with age. 完全对应，选 C。\nD) 偷换概念\n原文 Children's dream life…seems to be similar to their waking imagination and\nnarration 的相似对象是 \"清醒时的想象与叙事\"，They reflect kids' waking life. 把 \"想\n象/叙事\" 偷换成 \"waking life\"（清醒生活本身）。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2025-12-2-r-55", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 55, "stem": "What can we assume about babies who appear upset upon waking?", "options": ["A) They have been roused against their will.", "B) They have been disturbed by a nightmare.", "C) They have trouble recollecting the images of their dreams.", "D) They have been affected by something other than dreaming."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （推断题）\n【判型】What can we assume about babies who appear upset upon waking? 含 assume，按\n推断题处理。\n【拆句】Q55 结构是 \"What can we assume about babies who appear upset upon waking?\"，\nwho 引导定语从句修饰 babies，upon waking 是时间状语 \"一醒来时\"，整句无否定无比\n较，理解关键是把 who 从句翻成 \"那些一醒来就显得不安的婴儿\"。\n【定位】So, if your baby seems to be in a stressful state while sleeping or is upset upon waking, there\nmay be other factors at play.\n因此，如果你的婴儿睡觉时显得紧张、或一醒来就不安，那很可能是别的因素在起作用。\n【选项】A) 无中生有\n原文 P7 没有说婴儿被人弄醒；They have been roused against their will. 凭空添加\n\"被弄醒\" + \"违背意愿\" 两个文本无据的语义。\nB) 与原文相反\n原文 babies do not have nightmares 明确说婴儿不做噩梦；They have been\ndisturbed by a nightmare. 反着说婴儿被噩梦惊扰。\nC) 答非所问\n题问 \"哭醒原因\"，They have trouble recollecting the images of their dreams. 把焦\n点换成 \"回忆梦境困难\"，且 25% of the kids in Foulkes' studies had no recollection\nof dreaming 说的是 5-9 岁孩子，而非 \"babies\"。\nD) ✓ 正确\nthere may be other factors at play ↔\nThey have been affected by something other than dreaming.\n预判 \"原因不是做梦\" 与 They have been affected by something other than\ndreaming. 完全对应，选 D。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "推断题"]}]});
